        <div id="articles">

          <article class="doc-article" id="introduction" data-group="Getting Started" data-title="Introduction">
            <h1>Testimonials Block: Introduction</h1>
            <p class="lead">Testimonials Block turns customer reviews into social proof you can drop on any page. One block holds 39 layouts: grids, sliders, marquees, speech bubbles, timelines, review score badges, video and audio reviews, a submission form and an NPS poll.</p>
            <h2 id="intro-what">What You Get</h2>
            <ul><li><strong>39 layout blocks</strong> in one plugin, all sharing the same review data and the same settings sidebar.</li><li><strong>A Testimonials post type</strong>, so you write a review once and reuse it in every block on the site.</li><li><strong>A front-end form</strong> that saves visitor reviews as pending, for you to approve before anyone sees them.</li><li><strong>Score badges</strong> styled after Google, Trustpilot, G2, Facebook and Capterra, plus a verified buyer seal.</li><li><strong>A Feedback &amp; NPS Poll</strong> block with its own results screen in the admin.</li><li><strong>Responsive controls</strong> for columns and gaps, set separately for desktop, tablet and mobile.</li></ul>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="image" data-src="https://ps.w.org/b-testimonials-block/assets/screenshot-1.png" data-caption="A published testimonials grid: photo, name, designation, star rating, review text and an Expand button"></figure>
            <h2 id="intro-structure">How the Blocks Fit Together</h2>
            <p>Only one block appears in the inserter: <strong>Testimonials</strong>. The other 39 are its children, so you insert the parent once and then choose which layout it renders. Switching layout later keeps your reviews, which is why the plugin is built this way.</p>
            <p>Every block reads the same five pieces of review data, so the same content works in a grid, a slider or a badge:</p>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Review field</th><th>Where it comes from</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Photo</strong></td><td>The card image, or the featured image of a testimonial post</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Name</strong></td><td>The card name field, or the testimonial post title</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Designation</strong></td><td>The card designation field, or the post’s Designation meta</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Review text</strong></td><td>The card review field, or the post content</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rating</strong></td><td>A number from 0 to 5, in steps of 0.1</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="intro-limits">What This Plugin Does Not Do</h2>
            <p>Being clear about this saves you a support ticket:</p>
            <ul><li><strong>There is no shortcode.</strong> The plugin registers blocks only. See <a href="#usage-elsewhere" data-nav>Using the Blocks Outside the Block Editor</a> for Elementor and PHP.</li><li><strong>Reviews are not imported from Google, Trustpilot, G2, Facebook or Capterra.</strong> The badge blocks are styled to look like those services, and you type the score and count yourself.</li><li><strong>The brand logos on those five badges cannot be swapped or recoloured.</strong> They are trademarks. You can change their size.</li><li><strong>Everything is free.</strong> This version has no Pro tier, no licence key and no locked features.</li></ul>
            <h2 id="intro-next">Where to Go Next</h2>
            <ul><li><a href="#installation" data-nav>Installation &amp; Activation</a> if you have not installed the plugin yet.</li><li><a href="#quick-start" data-nav>Quick Start</a> to publish your first section in about five minutes.</li><li><a href="#block-library" data-nav>All 39 Blocks at a Glance</a> to see what is on offer.</li><li><a href="https://wordpress.org/plugins/b-testimonials-block/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The plugin on WordPress.org</a> and <a href="https://bplugins.com/support/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">bPlugins support</a>.</li></ul>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="installation" data-group="Getting Started" data-title="Installation & Activation">
            <h1>Installation & Activation</h1>
            <p class="lead">Testimonials Block installs like any other WordPress plugin, and needs no licence key or setup wizard.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="video" data-src="" data-caption="Video walkthrough - searching for B Testimonials Block in Plugins &gt; Add New Plugin, installing it, activating it, and landing on the Testimonials screen the plugin opens after activation"></figure>
            <h2 id="install-requirements">Requirements</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Minimum</th></tr><tr><td>WordPress</td><td>6.5 or newer</td></tr><tr><td>PHP</td><td>7.4 or newer</td></tr><tr><td>Editor</td><td>The block editor (Gutenberg), which ships with WordPress</td></tr><tr><td>Browser</td><td>Any current version of Chrome, Firefox, Safari or Edge</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>The block editor is required to build a section. Your visitors need nothing special, and the published output works on classic themes and block themes alike.</p></div>
            <h2 id="install-dashboard">Install from the WordPress Dashboard</h2>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Go to <strong>Plugins &rarr; Add New Plugin</strong>.</li><li>Search for <strong>B Testimonials Block</strong>.</li><li>Click <strong>Install Now</strong>, then <strong>Activate</strong>.</li></ol>
            <h2 id="install-zip">Install by Uploading the ZIP</h2>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Download <code>b-testimonials-block.zip</code> from <a href="https://wordpress.org/plugins/b-testimonials-block/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the plugin page on WordPress.org</a>.</li><li>Go to <strong>Plugins &rarr; Add New Plugin &rarr; Upload Plugin</strong> and choose the file.</li><li>Click <strong>Install Now</strong>, then <strong>Activate</strong>.</li></ol>
            <p>You can also copy the unzipped folder to <code>/wp-content/plugins/</code> over FTP and activate it from the <strong>Plugins</strong> screen.</p>
            <h2 id="install-after">What Happens on Activation</h2>
            <p>The plugin sends you straight to the <strong>Testimonials</strong> screen the first time it is activated, so you land where the reviews live. That redirect happens once, and it is skipped when you activate several plugins at the same time or use WP-CLI.</p>
            <p>A <strong>Testimonials</strong> menu appears in the admin sidebar with four items:</p>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Menu item</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td><strong>All Testimonials</strong></td><td>Your reviews, stored as posts. See <a href="#testimonials-cpt" data-nav>Managing Testimonials in One Place</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Add New</strong></td><td>Writes a new review.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Submissions</strong></td><td>Reviews sent in by visitors, waiting for approval. See <a href="#submissions" data-nav>Reviewing Customer Submissions</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Feedback &amp; NPS Poll</strong></td><td>Poll results and score bands. See <a href="#nps-dashboard" data-nav>Feedback &amp; NPS Poll Results</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Demo &amp; Help</strong></td><td>Live demos of every block, the block on/off switches, and settings. See <a href="#dashboard" data-nav>Demo &amp; Help Dashboard</a>.</td></tr></table></div>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="image" data-src="img/docs/01-testimonials-admin-menu.png" data-caption="The Testimonials menu in the WordPress admin sidebar, expanded to show All Testimonials, Add New, Submissions, Feedback &amp; NPS Poll and Demo &amp; Help. 1200px wide, #0852c2 canvas, Screely frame, red #ef4444 arrow on the menu, TinyPNG"></figure>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Nothing else is configured for you. There is no licence key to enter, and the blocks work the moment the plugin is active.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="quick-start" data-group="Getting Started" data-title="Quick Start: Your First Section">
            <h1>Quick Start: Your First Section</h1>
            <p class="lead">This is the whole flow, from an empty page to a published wall of reviews. It takes about five minutes and no code.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="video" data-src="" data-caption="Video walkthrough - inserting the Testimonials block on a page, picking Centered Cards Grid from the placeholder, editing the three demo cards, and publishing the page"></figure>
            <h2 id="qs-insert">Step 1 - Insert the Testimonials Block</h2>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Edit any post or page in the block editor.</li><li>Click the <strong>+</strong> button and search for <strong>Testimonials</strong>, or type <code>/b testimonials</code> in an empty paragraph and press Enter.</li><li>The block lands on the canvas showing a layout picker rather than a finished design.</li></ol>
            <p>The block is filed under the <strong>bPlugins</strong> category in the inserter, alongside the other bPlugins blocks on your site.</p>
            <h2 id="qs-layout">Step 2 - Pick a Layout</h2>
            <p>The picker is headed <strong>Select Your Testimonial Block</strong> and shows the popular layouts as cards. Click one to use it.</p>
            <p>If none of them is right, click <strong>View All 40+ Layouts &amp; Widgets in Modal Popup</strong> to open the full switcher, or <strong>Use Classic Single Block Slider</strong> to keep the original single block.</p>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Pick anything for now. Layouts are interchangeable, so you can switch later without retyping a single review. See <a href="#block-switcher" data-nav>Switching Layouts</a>.</p></div>
            <h2 id="qs-content">Step 3 - Enter Your Reviews</h2>
            <p>Every layout arrives with sample cards so you can see the design working. Replace them in the block sidebar, on the <strong>General</strong> tab.</p>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Open the panel named after the block, for example <strong>Add New Item</strong>, and click a card row to expand it.</li><li>Fill in <strong>Name</strong>, <strong>Designation</strong>, <strong>Review</strong> and <strong>Rating</strong>, and upload an <strong>Image</strong> for the photo.</li><li>Click <strong>Add New Card</strong> for the next review, or <strong>Duplicate</strong> to copy the one you just wrote.</li><li>Remove the cards you do not need with <strong>Remove</strong>.</li></ol>
            <p>Reusing the same reviews in several places? Store them in the Testimonials post type instead and set <strong>Content Source</strong> to <strong>Testimonials (CPT)</strong>. See <a href="#content-source" data-nav>Content Source</a>.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="image" data-src="https://ps.w.org/b-testimonials-block/assets/screenshot-2.png" data-caption="The block sidebar: General and Style tabs, the card editor with Name, Designation, Review and Rating, the Elements toggles, and the Layout, Card, Image and typography panels"></figure>
            <h2 id="qs-style">Step 4 - Fit It to Your Design</h2>
            <ul><li>On the <strong>General</strong> tab, set <strong>Columns</strong>, <strong>Column Gap</strong> and <strong>Row Gap</strong> in the <strong>Layout</strong> panel.</li><li>On the <strong>Style</strong> tab, set the card background, border and shadow in the <strong>Card</strong> panel.</li><li>Set the fonts and colours for the name, designation and review text in their own panels.</li></ul>
            <p>Only the controls that change something on the layout you picked are shown, so a timeline offers no column count and a video block offers no star colour. See <a href="#style-tab" data-nav>The Style Tab</a>.</p>
            <h2 id="qs-publish">Step 5 - Publish and Check</h2>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Click <strong>Publish</strong> or <strong>Update</strong>.</li><li>Open the page on the front end.</li><li>Resize the browser window, or use the editor’s tablet and mobile previews, to confirm the column counts you set.</li></ol>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Clear your site cache after publishing if you use a caching plugin such as WP Rocket or LiteSpeed Cache. A cached page can keep serving the old markup.</p></div>
            <h2 id="qs-success">Success Check</h2>
            <p>Your section is working when all of these are true on the published page:</p>
            <ul><li>Every review shows its photo, name, designation, text and stars.</li><li>The number of columns matches what you set for the device you are viewing on.</li><li>Sliders move, marquees scroll, and the <strong>Expand</strong> button opens a truncated review.</li></ul>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="block-switcher" data-group="Getting Started" data-title="Switching Layouts">
            <h1>Switching Layouts</h1>
            <p class="lead">The block switcher moves your reviews from one layout to another without you retyping them. It is the fastest way to try the library.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="video" data-src="" data-caption="Video walkthrough - opening Change Block / Layout, searching the switcher modal, filtering by the four category chips, and switching a live grid to Speech Bubble Cards without losing its reviews"></figure>
            <h2 id="bs-open">Open the Switcher</h2>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Select the block on the canvas.</li><li>In the sidebar, open the <strong>Select / Switch Block</strong> panel.</li><li>Click <strong>Change Block / Layout</strong>.</li></ol>
            <p>The modal is headed <strong>Testimonial Block Switcher</strong>, with the subtitle <em>Select from 40+ modern layouts &amp; social proof widgets</em>.</p>
            <h2 id="bs-find">Find a Layout</h2>
            <p>The modal gives you two ways to narrow the list:</p>
            <ul><li>The <strong>Search layouts</strong> field, which matches the layout name and its description.</li><li>Category chips: <strong>All Blocks</strong>, <strong>Grid &amp; Layouts</strong>, <strong>Trust &amp; Badges</strong>, <strong>Media &amp; Audio</strong> and <strong>Forms &amp; Polls</strong>.</li></ul>
            <p>The layout you are using now carries an <strong>Active</strong> pill and its button reads <strong>Currently Selected</strong>. Every other card offers <strong>Use This Layout</strong>.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="image" data-src="img/docs/02-block-switcher-modal.png" data-caption="The Testimonial Block Switcher modal: search field, the five category chips, a grid of layout cards, and the Active pill on the current layout. 1200px wide, #0852c2 canvas, Screely frame, red #ef4444 arrows on the chips and Use This Layout, TinyPNG"></figure>
            <h2 id="bs-carry">What Carries Over</h2>
            <p>Your review cards, their photos and their ratings are shared by every layout, so they survive a switch. Settings that only one layout has do not.</p>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Survives a switch?</th></tr><tr><td>Review cards, photos, names, designations, ratings</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Content Source and its query</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Card, typography and colour values</td><td>Yes, where the new layout paints the same part</td></tr><tr><td>Slider, marquee and accordion options</td><td>Only if the new layout has that panel</td></tr><tr><td>Badge title, score and count text</td><td>Yes, the badges share those four fields</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>A value that has no home on the new layout is kept in the block, not deleted. Switch back and it is still there.</p></div>
            <h2 id="bs-modes">The Two Other Buttons</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Switch to 40+ Layout Placeholder</strong></td><td>Empties the block back to the layout picker, so you choose again from scratch.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Switch to Classic Single Block Mode</strong></td><td>Returns to the original single Testimonials block, the one the plugin shipped with in 1.0.0.</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>A layout switched off on the All Blocks screen does not appear in the switcher. If one is missing, check <a href="#all-blocks" data-nav>Turning Blocks On and Off</a>.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="testimonials-cpt" data-group="Managing Reviews" data-title="Managing Testimonials in One Place">
            <h1>Managing Testimonials in One Place</h1>
            <p class="lead">The Testimonials post type is the central store for your reviews. Write a review once, then show it in as many blocks as you like.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="video" data-src="" data-caption="Video walkthrough - adding a testimonial under Testimonials &gt; Add New, filling the title, review, rating, designation and company, setting the featured image, and seeing it in the All Testimonials list"></figure>
            <h2 id="cpt-add">Add a Testimonial</h2>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Go to <strong>Testimonials &rarr; Add New</strong>.</li><li>Put the reviewer’s <strong>name</strong> in the title field.</li><li>Put the <strong>review itself</strong> in the main content area.</li><li>Fill in <strong>Rating</strong>, <strong>Designation</strong> and <strong>Company</strong> in the <strong>Testimonial Details</strong> box on the right.</li><li>Set a <strong>Featured image</strong> for the reviewer’s photo.</li><li>Click <strong>Publish</strong>.</li></ol>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>Testimonials open in the classic editor on purpose. The screen is a title, a review and three short fields, and the classic editor puts all of them in front of you at once.</p></div>
            <h2 id="cpt-fields">The Testimonial Details Fields</h2>
            <p>The three fields in the Testimonial Details box, and how blocks read them.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Rating (0-5)</code></td><td>number</td><td>5</td><td>The star rating, in steps of 0.1, so 4.5 stars is allowed.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Designation</code></td><td>text</td><td>None</td><td>The line under the name, for example Head of Product.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Company</code></td><td>text</td><td>None</td><td>Stored with the review. Blocks that print one line under the name use Designation.</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>A rating typed as 4.5 stays 4.5. Earlier versions rounded it down to 4, so re-save any half-star review you entered before 1.0.3.</p></div>
            <h2 id="cpt-list">The Testimonials List</h2>
            <p>The <strong>All Testimonials</strong> list adds two columns next to the title, so you can scan your reviews without opening them:</p>
            <ul><li><strong>Designation</strong>, exactly as you typed it.</li><li><strong>Rating</strong>, drawn as stars.</li></ul>
            <p>Testimonials are not public pages. They have no permalink, no archive and no front-end template of their own: they exist to feed the blocks.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="image" data-src="img/docs/03-all-testimonials-list.png" data-caption="The All Testimonials list showing the Title, Designation and Rating columns with several published reviews. 1200px wide, #0852c2 canvas, Screely frame, red #ef4444 arrow on the Rating column, TinyPNG"></figure>
            <h2 id="cpt-use">Use Them in a Block</h2>
            <p>Insert any layout, then set <strong>Content Source</strong> to <strong>Testimonials (CPT)</strong>. The block fills itself from this list and stays current as you add reviews. See <a href="#content-source" data-nav>Content Source</a> for the query options.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="content-source" data-group="Managing Reviews" data-title="Content Source: Manual or CPT">
            <h1>Content Source: Manual or CPT</h1>
            <p class="lead">Every layout can take its reviews from the block itself or from the Testimonials post type. The choice lives in one panel and can be changed at any time.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="video" data-src="" data-caption="Video walkthrough - switching a block from Manual to Testimonials (CPT) and setting Number, Order By and Order so the block fills itself from the post type"></figure>
            <h2 id="cs-which">Which One to Use</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Source</th><th>Best for</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Manual</strong></td><td>Reviews used on one page only, and copy you want to write inside the block.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Testimonials (CPT)</strong></td><td>Reviews reused across the site, and lists that should update themselves as new reviews arrive.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="cs-switch">Switch to the Post Type</h2>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Select the block and open the <strong>General</strong> tab.</li><li>Open the <strong>Content Source</strong> panel.</li><li>Set <strong>Source</strong> to <strong>Testimonials (CPT)</strong>.</li><li>Set <strong>Number</strong>, <strong>Order By</strong> and <strong>Order</strong>.</li></ol>
            <p>The query controls, shown only when Source is set to Testimonials (CPT).</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Number</code></td><td>number</td><td>6</td><td>How many published testimonials to show.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Order By</code></td><td>select</td><td>Date</td><td>Date, Title or Menu Order.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Order</code></td><td>select</td><td>Descending</td><td>Descending or Ascending.</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>Only published testimonials are pulled in, so a pending submission never appears on the front end until you approve it.</p>
            <h2 id="cs-cards">What Happens to Your Manual Cards</h2>
            <p>They are kept. Switching to <strong>Testimonials (CPT)</strong> hides the card editor and renders posts instead, and switching back to <strong>Manual</strong> brings your cards back exactly as they were.</p>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>With <strong>Testimonials (CPT)</strong> selected, the reviews are fetched when the page renders. Editing a testimonial updates every block using it, with no need to open those pages.</p></div>
            <h2 id="cs-menu-order">A Note on Menu Order</h2>
            <p><strong>Order By</strong> offers <strong>Menu Order</strong>, but the testimonial editor has no order field of its own, so every testimonial sits at zero and the order falls back to the date. Only a developer setting <code>menu_order</code> directly can use it.</p>
            <p>To control the sequence yourself today, use <strong>Manual</strong> cards and drag them into place, or name your testimonials so that <strong>Title</strong> order gives you the sequence you want.</p>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Set <strong>Number</strong> to more reviews than you need on a slider or marquee. Both keep moving, so a longer list means less obvious repetition.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="review-cards" data-group="Managing Reviews" data-title="Adding and Editing Review Cards">
            <h1>Adding and Editing Review Cards</h1>
            <p class="lead">With <strong>Content Source</strong> set to Manual, your reviews live in the block. The card editor is one panel on the General tab.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="video" data-src="" data-caption="Video walkthrough - adding, duplicating, reordering and removing review cards in the Add New Item panel, including uploading a photo and setting a rating"></figure>
            <h2 id="rc-editor">The Card Editor</h2>
            <p>Cards are listed as numbered rows, with one open at a time so the sidebar stays readable. Click a row to open it.</p>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it sets</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Image</strong></td><td>The reviewer’s photo. Upload a file or paste a URL.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Name</strong></td><td>The reviewer’s name.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Designation</strong></td><td>The line under the name.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Review</strong></td><td>The review text itself.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rating</strong></td><td>Stars, from 0 to 5.</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>Some layouts add a field of their own to the same rows. The Audio Testimonials cards add an <strong>Audio File</strong> field, and the Customer Case Study cards add Challenge, Solution and Result sections.</p>
            <h2 id="rc-manage">Add, Copy and Remove</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Add New Card</strong></td><td>Appends an empty card at the end of the list.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Duplicate</strong></td><td>Copies the open card, including its photo and rating.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Remove</strong></td><td>Deletes the open card.</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Build one card exactly as you want it, then use <strong>Duplicate</strong> for the rest and change only the text. It is quicker than starting each card from empty.</p></div>
            <h2 id="rc-elements">Choose Which Parts Show</h2>
            <p>You do not have to fill every field. The <strong>Elements</strong> panel switches each card part on and off for the whole block, so a layout can show names and reviews with no photos at all. See <a href="#elements-excerpt" data-nav>Elements, Excerpt and Expand</a>.</p>
            <h2 id="rc-single">Single-Widget Blocks</h2>
            <p>Sixteen blocks show one widget rather than a list of cards, so they have no card editor. They take their text from a panel named after the block instead, for example <strong>Google Review Badge Settings</strong> or <strong>Poll Settings</strong>.</p>
            <p>Those blocks are the five score badges, the verified buyer seal, the floating review badge, Rating Summary, Star Rating Progress Bars, Testimonial Stats, Trust Badges, the Testimonial Form, the Feedback &amp; NPS Poll, the Before / After slider, the Comparison Review Table and the FAQ Review Accordion.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="submissions" data-group="Managing Reviews" data-title="Reviewing Customer Submissions">
            <h1>Reviewing Customer Submissions</h1>
            <p class="lead">Reviews sent in through the Testimonial Form block are saved as pending. Nothing a visitor writes appears on your site until you approve it.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="video" data-src="" data-caption="Video walkthrough - a visitor submitting the Testimonial Form on the front end, then an administrator approving it under Testimonials &gt; Submissions and seeing it appear in a block"></figure>
            <h2 id="sub-screen">The Submissions Screen</h2>
            <p>Go to <strong>Testimonials &rarr; Submissions</strong>. The submenu carries a count bubble whenever something is waiting, and every other admin screen shows a notice with a <strong>View Submissions</strong> button until you clear the queue.</p>
            <p>Four filters group the queue by state:</p>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Filter</th><th>Shows</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Pending</strong></td><td>New submissions waiting for a decision. This is the default view.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Approved</strong></td><td>Submissions you published. These are now ordinary testimonials.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Trash</strong></td><td>Submissions you rejected.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>All</strong></td><td>Pending and approved together.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="sub-actions">Approve, Reject or Delete</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Action</th><th>What happens</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Approve</strong></td><td>Publishes the review. Any block set to <strong>Testimonials (CPT)</strong> can now show it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reject</strong></td><td>Moves it to the trash, where you can still recover it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Delete</strong></td><td>Removes it permanently. This cannot be undone.</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>Tick several rows and use the bulk action select to approve, trash or delete them in one go. The list shows 20 submissions per page.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="image" data-src="img/docs/04-submissions-screen.png" data-caption="The Submissions screen with the Pending filter active, a submitted review card, and the Approve, Reject and Delete actions. 1200px wide, #0852c2 canvas, Screely frame, red #ef4444 arrow on Approve, TinyPNG"></figure>
            <h2 id="sub-contents">What a Submission Contains</h2>
            <p>The form stores exactly what the visitor filled in, mapped onto the same fields you use by hand:</p>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Form field</th><th>Stored as</th></tr><tr><td>Name</td><td>The testimonial title</td></tr><tr><td>Review</td><td>The testimonial content</td></tr><tr><td>Rating</td><td>The rating meta, clamped to 1 to 5</td></tr><tr><td>Designation and Company</td><td>Their own meta fields</td></tr><tr><td>Email</td><td>A meta field, never shown on the front end</td></tr><tr><td>Photo</td><td>A JPG, PNG, GIF or WebP upload, set as the featured image</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>Approving a submission is the same as publishing a testimonial, so you can edit it first. Open it from <strong>All Testimonials</strong>, fix a typo or trim the length, then publish.</p></div>
            <div class="callout warn"><span class="co-label">Important</span><p>The form accepts anonymous submissions by design, since it is meant for real customers who are not logged in. Check the queue regularly, and never approve a review without reading it.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="block-library" data-group="Block Library" data-title="All 39 Blocks at a Glance">
            <h1>All 39 Blocks at a Glance</h1>
            <p class="lead">One parent block, 39 layouts. This page lists every one of them with the category it sits in, so you can find the right layout before you start building.</p>
            <h2 id="lib-how">How to Read This List</h2>
            <p>The categories are the chips in the block switcher modal. A layout appears in the switcher only while it is switched on for the site.</p>
            <ul><li><strong>Grid &amp; Layouts</strong> shows several reviews at once, in a grid, list, slider or moving strip.</li><li><strong>Trust &amp; Badges</strong> shows a score, a seal or a statistic rather than full reviews.</li><li><strong>Media &amp; Audio</strong> plays or compares media.</li><li><strong>Forms &amp; Polls</strong> asks your visitor for something.</li></ul>
            <h2 id="lib-layouts">Grid & Layouts</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Block</th><th>What it shows</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Testimonials Slider</strong></td><td>A touch-friendly carousel with navigation dots.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Testimonials List</strong></td><td>One review per full-width row.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Testimonials Masonry</strong></td><td>A staggered grid for cards of different heights.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Testimonials Marquee</strong></td><td>A strip of reviews scrolling without a break.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Centered Cards Grid</strong></td><td>A card grid with the photo and name centred above the review.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gradient Border Grid</strong></td><td>Cards with a gradient border and a star badge in one corner.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Coverflow Carousel</strong></td><td>A slider that pushes the side cards back in 3D around the centre one.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3D Flip Perspective Carousel</strong></td><td>A carousel whose cards flip on a perspective axis.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Compact Reviews List</strong></td><td>A dense list for sidebars and footers.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Minimalist Reviews Grid</strong></td><td>Monochrome cards that lift slightly on hover.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Avatar Reviews List</strong></td><td>Rows led by a large photo, for spotlighting people.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Quote Box Showcase</strong></td><td>A big quote mark on a gradient card with a coloured left bar.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Speech Bubble Cards</strong></td><td>Chat bubbles with a tail you can position and colour.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Customer Journey Timeline</strong></td><td>A vertical rail with a dot per review.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Stacked Review Cards</strong></td><td>An overlapping deck you step through with dots and arrows.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Customer Case Study</strong></td><td>A long card with Challenge, Solution and Result sections plus metrics.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hero Testimonial Spotlight</strong></td><td>A full-width banner built around one quote.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="lib-social">Trust & Badges</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Block</th><th>What it shows</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Google Reviews Badge</strong></td><td>A Google Business style score card.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Trustpilot Score Badge</strong></td><td>A Trustpilot style TrustScore and review count.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>G2 Review Badge</strong></td><td>A G2 style score with a category line.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Facebook Recommendation Badge</strong></td><td>A Facebook page recommendation summary.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Capterra Score Badge</strong></td><td>A Capterra style software rating card.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Verified Buyer Trust Seal</strong></td><td>A tick, a heading and a line of reassurance. No score.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Floating Review Badge</strong></td><td>A compact score badge for a page corner.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rating Summary</strong></td><td>One average score, its stars, and a review count line.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Star Rating Progress Bars</strong></td><td>A five-row breakdown of how many reviews gave each score.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Testimonial Stats</strong></td><td>Animated number counters, for example 5,000+ happy clients.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Trust Badges</strong></td><td>A row of badges, each with an icon, a title and a subtitle.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Client Logos</strong></td><td>A trusted-by logo grid, greyscale until hover.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Social Proof Toast</strong></td><td>A small notification that rotates through recent reviews.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Floating Avatar Bubbles</strong></td><td>Photo bubbles that reveal a quote when you point at one.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="lib-media">Media & Audio</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Block</th><th>What it shows</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Video Testimonials</strong></td><td>Video reviews that open in a lightbox. YouTube, Vimeo or MP4.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Audio Testimonials</strong></td><td>Voice note reviews with a waveform style player.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Before &amp; After</strong></td><td>Two images and a slider handle to compare them.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="lib-interactive">Forms & Polls</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Block</th><th>What it shows</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Testimonial Form</strong></td><td>A front-end form that saves reviews as pending.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Feedback &amp; NPS Poll</strong></td><td>A numbered scale question with its own results screen.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Popup Modal Review Trigger</strong></td><td>A badge or button that opens a full review in a modal.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Comparison Review Table</strong></td><td>A side-by-side table of customers, ratings and reviews.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>FAQ Review Accordion</strong></td><td>Collapsible question and answer rows.</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Try before you build. <strong>Testimonials &rarr; Demo &amp; Help &rarr; Demos</strong> opens a live preview of any block on your own site, with your own theme applied.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="grid-blocks" data-group="Block Library" data-title="Grids, Lists and Masonry">
            <h1>Grids, Lists and Masonry</h1>
            <p class="lead">The layouts that put several reviews on screen at once. They share the same controls, so learning one teaches you all of them.</p>
            <h2 id="grid-blocks-list">The Blocks</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Block</th><th>Use it when</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Centered Cards Grid</strong></td><td>You want a tidy, symmetrical wall of reviews.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gradient Border Grid</strong></td><td>The section needs more colour, with a star badge per card.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Minimalist Reviews Grid</strong></td><td>The page is already busy and the reviews should stay quiet.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Testimonials Masonry</strong></td><td>Your reviews are different lengths and equal-height cards waste space.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Testimonials List</strong></td><td>Each review deserves a full-width row.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Compact Reviews List</strong></td><td>The space is narrow, such as a sidebar or footer.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Avatar Reviews List</strong></td><td>The person matters as much as the review.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Quote Box Showcase</strong></td><td>You want a magazine pull-quote look.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Speech Bubble Cards</strong></td><td>The reviews should read as conversation.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="grid-columns">Setting the Columns</h2>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Open the <strong>General</strong> tab and the <strong>Layout</strong> panel.</li><li>Use the device switcher next to <strong>Columns</strong> to pick desktop, tablet or mobile.</li><li>Set the count for that device. The defaults are 3, 2 and 1.</li><li>Set <strong>Column Gap</strong> and <strong>Row Gap</strong> for the space between cards.</li></ol>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>Column and gap values are stored per device, so setting three columns on desktop does not touch what tablet and mobile show.</p></div>
            <h2 id="grid-tail">The Speech Bubble Tail</h2>
            <p>Speech Bubble Cards add a <strong>Speech Bubble</strong> panel on the Style tab, for the pointer on each bubble:</p>
            <p>The Speech Bubble panel.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Tail</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>Draws the pointer. Switch it off for a plain rounded card.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Tail Size (px)</code></td><td>number</td><td>12</td><td>How large the pointer is.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Tail Offset (px)</code></td><td>number</td><td>30</td><td>How far along the edge the pointer sits.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Tail Position</code></td><td>select</td><td>Left</td><td>Which end of the edge the pointer points from.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Tail Color</code></td><td>colour</td><td>Follows the card</td><td>The pointer fill. A Follow the card again button hands it back to the Card panel.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Tail Outline</code></td><td>colour</td><td>Follows the card</td><td>The pointer border, matched to the card border by default.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="grid-gradient">The Gradient Border</h2>
            <p>Gradient Border Grid adds a <strong>Gradient Border</strong> panel with <strong>Gradient border</strong>, <strong>Gradient Start</strong>, <strong>Gradient End</strong>, <strong>Angle (deg)</strong> and <strong>Border Width (px)</strong>, plus a <strong>Star badge</strong> group for the badge’s background, colour, position, size and radius.</p>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Set <strong>Gradient Start</strong> and <strong>Gradient End</strong> to two shades of one brand colour. Two unrelated colours draw attention to the border instead of the review.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="slider-blocks" data-group="Block Library" data-title="Sliders, Carousels and Marquee">
            <h1>Sliders, Carousels and Marquee</h1>
            <p class="lead">The layouts that move. Use them when you have more reviews than fit on screen and you would rather not make the page longer.</p>
            <h2 id="slider-blocks-list">The Blocks</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Block</th><th>Movement</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Testimonials Slider</strong></td><td>Slides one card set at a time, with arrows and dots.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Coverflow Carousel</strong></td><td>Centres one card and pushes its neighbours back in 3D.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3D Flip Perspective Carousel</strong></td><td>Flips cards on a perspective axis as they pass.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Testimonials Marquee</strong></td><td>Scrolls continuously in one direction, with no stops.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Stacked Review Cards</strong></td><td>Steps through an overlapping deck one card at a time.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Social Proof Toast</strong></td><td>Rotates through short notifications in a corner.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="slider-choose">Slider or Marquee?</h2>
            <ul><li>A <strong>slider</strong> holds each card still long enough to read. Use it for reviews of a few sentences.</li><li>A <strong>marquee</strong> never stops, so it reads as decoration. Use it for one-line quotes, and turn on <strong>Pause on hover</strong> so a visitor can stop to read.</li></ul>
            <h2 id="slider-arrangement">Same Reviews, Any Movement</h2>
            <p>You do not need a different block for each kind of movement. Any grid layout can be switched to <strong>Slider</strong>, <strong>Masonry</strong>, <strong>List</strong>, <strong>Marquee</strong>, <strong>3D Slider</strong> or <strong>Coverflow</strong> with the <strong>Arrangement</strong> control. See <a href="#layout-arrangement" data-nav>Layout, Arrangement and Themes</a>.</p>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>The controls follow the arrangement, not the block name. Choose <strong>Marquee</strong> on a grid block and the Marquee panel appears; choose <strong>Coverflow</strong> and the 3D controls appear.</p></div>
            <h2 id="slider-editor">Motion in the Editor</h2>
            <p>Constant movement makes a block hard to edit. Two toggles stop it while you work, without changing the published page:</p>
            <ul><li><strong>Pause while editing</strong>, on the Social Proof Toast’s Notification panel.</li><li><strong>Pause on hover</strong>, on the Marquee panel, which also helps your visitors.</li></ul>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Long reviews suit a slider; short punchy ones suit a marquee. If a marquee looks like it repeats too soon, add more reviews or raise <strong>Number</strong> on the Content Source panel.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="story-blocks" data-group="Block Library" data-title="Spotlight, Timeline and Case Study">
            <h1>Spotlight, Timeline and Case Study</h1>
            <p class="lead">The layouts built around a story rather than a wall of quotes. Use them where one customer is worth a whole section.</p>
            <h2 id="story-hero">Hero Testimonial Spotlight</h2>
            <p>A full-width banner built around a single quote, with the photo and details beside it. Set the block to <strong>Full width</strong> in the block toolbar to get the intended effect.</p>
            <p>The hero shows its column control only once there are more than two reviews in the block, so the sidebar never offers a setting that would move nothing.</p>
            <h2 id="story-timeline">Customer Journey Timeline</h2>
            <p>A vertical rail with a dot per review, read top to bottom. Use it for a story that happened in stages, such as onboarding, first results, and renewal.</p>
            <p>On the Style tab, the <strong>Colors</strong> panel names the rail parts for what they are: <strong>Timeline Rail Start &amp; Dots</strong> and <strong>Timeline Rail End</strong>, which are the two ends of the rail gradient.</p>
            <h2 id="story-case">Customer Case Study</h2>
            <p>The longest card in the library. Each card carries the reviewer’s details plus three labelled sections, <strong>Challenge</strong>, <strong>Solution</strong> and <strong>Result</strong>, and a metric row.</p>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Keep each section to two or three sentences. A case study card is read, not skimmed, but only if it looks readable.</p></div>
            <h2 id="story-stack">Stacked Review Cards</h2>
            <p>An overlapping deck that shows one review at a time. It has its own two controls in the <strong>Autoplay</strong> panel:</p>
            <p>The Stacked Review Cards autoplay controls.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Autoplay</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>Off</td><td>Advances the deck on its own.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Delay (seconds)</code></td><td>number</td><td>5</td><td>How long each card stays on top.</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>Its prev and next buttons and its dots only exist when there is more than one review, so the <strong>Nav Buttons &amp; Active Dot</strong> and <strong>Nav Dots</strong> colours appear only once a second review is added.</p>
            <h2 id="story-popup">Popup Modal Review Trigger</h2>
            <p>A compact badge or button that opens the full review in a modal, for pages with no room for a review section. The <strong>Popup</strong> panel styles the modal itself: <strong>Overlay Color</strong>, <strong>Overlay Blur (px)</strong>, <strong>Panel Background</strong>, <strong>Max Width (px)</strong>, <strong>Panel Padding</strong>, <strong>Panel Radius (px)</strong>, the close button size and colour, and the avatar, name, designation, stars and review text sizes.</p>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>Before version 1.0.4 the modal was built from fixed inline styles, so a dark trigger card opened a white popup. Every part of it is a control now.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="badge-blocks" data-group="Block Library" data-title="Review Score Badges">
            <h1>Review Score Badges</h1>
            <p class="lead">Five badges styled after the review sites your customers recognise, plus a compact floating badge. You type the score, so they work whatever your review history is.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="video" data-src="" data-caption="Video walkthrough - inserting the Google Reviews Badge, filling Badge Title, Rating Score and Review Count, sizing the logo and stars, and centring the badge in its column"></figure>
            <h2 id="badge-list">The Badges</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Block</th><th>Its settings panel</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Google Reviews Badge</strong></td><td>Google Review Badge Settings</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Trustpilot Score Badge</strong></td><td>Trustpilot Badge Settings</td></tr><tr><td><strong>G2 Review Badge</strong></td><td>G2 Badge Settings</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Facebook Recommendation Badge</strong></td><td>Facebook Review Badge Settings</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Capterra Score Badge</strong></td><td>Capterra Rating Badge Settings</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Floating Review Badge</strong></td><td>Review Badge Widget Settings</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Verified Buyer Trust Seal</strong></td><td>Verified Buyer Badge Settings</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="badge-fill">Fill In a Badge</h2>
            <p>Each badge asks for the same shape of information under slightly different labels, because each service words it differently:</p>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Badge</th><th>Fields it asks for</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Google</strong></td><td>Badge Title, Rating Score (4.9), Review Count (128+ Reviews)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Trustpilot</strong></td><td>Badge Title, TrustScore (4.9 / 5), Review Count (TrustScore | 500+ Reviews)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>G2</strong></td><td>Badge Title, Rating Score (4.8 / 5), Category / Text (Leader Category 2026)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Facebook</strong></td><td>Badge Title, Rating Score (5.0), Recommendation Text (Recommended by 250+ Customers)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Capterra</strong></td><td>Badge Title, Rating Score (4.8), Review Count / Text</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Floating Review Badge</strong></td><td>Widget Title, Rating Score, Review Count (Based on 320+ reviews)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Verified Buyer</strong></td><td>Badge Title and Description. No score.</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout warn"><span class="co-label">Important</span><p>These badges display what you type. They do not connect to Google, Trustpilot, G2, Facebook or Capterra, and nothing is fetched or verified. Keep the numbers honest and update them when your real scores change.</p></div>
            <h2 id="badge-size">Size the Logo and Stars</h2>
            <p>The Google, Capterra, Facebook, Trustpilot and G2 marks are those companies’ trademarks, so they cannot be swapped or recoloured. Their size is yours: use <strong>Logo Size (px)</strong> in the <strong>Badge Logo</strong> panel.</p>
            <p>The six badges that show a score also get a <strong>Badge Score</strong> panel, with <strong>Typography</strong> for the score and <strong>Stars Size (px)</strong> for the star row.</p>
            <h2 id="badge-align">Position the Badge</h2>
            <p>A badge is narrower than the column it sits in, so it would otherwise sit against the left edge. Use <strong>Alignment</strong> in the <strong>Width &amp; Height</strong> panel to set <strong>Left</strong>, <strong>Center</strong> or <strong>Right</strong>.</p>
            <p>The same control is offered on the Social Proof Toast, for the same reason.</p>
            <h2 id="badge-typo">Name the Text Panels</h2>
            <p>On a badge, the shared <strong>Name</strong> and <strong>Designation</strong> typography panels are renamed for what they actually move: <strong>Badge Title</strong> and <strong>Review Count</strong>. The verified buyer seal names its second one <strong>Description</strong>, because it prints a sentence rather than a count.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="trust-blocks" data-group="Block Library" data-title="Trust Seals, Ratings and Stats">
            <h1>Trust Seals, Ratings and Stats</h1>
            <p class="lead">Widgets that summarise your reputation in a number, a bar or a badge row, instead of quoting anybody.</p>
            <h2 id="trust-summary">Rating Summary</h2>
            <p>One headline score with its stars and a count line. Its <strong>Rating Summary Settings</strong> panel asks for <strong>Overall Rating Score</strong> and <strong>Review Count Text</strong>.</p>
            <p>Rating Summary values.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>rating</code></td><td>number</td><td>4.8</td><td>The average score shown.</td></tr><tr><td><code>outOf</code></td><td>number</td><td>5</td><td>The scale the score is out of.</td></tr><tr><td><code>count</code></td><td>number</td><td>1234</td><td>How many reviews the average is based on.</td></tr><tr><td><code>countText</code></td><td>text</td><td>Based on {count} reviews</td><td>The count line. {count} is replaced with the number.</td></tr><tr><td><code>stacked</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>Off</td><td>Stacks the score above the stars instead of beside them.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="trust-bars">Star Rating Progress Bars</h2>
            <p>A five-row breakdown showing how many reviews gave each score. Fill in <strong>5-Star Count</strong> down to <strong>1-Star Count</strong>, and the bars work out their own widths.</p>
            <p>Use <strong>Section Title</strong> for the heading above the rows.</p>
            <h2 id="trust-stats">Testimonial Stats</h2>
            <p>Number counters that animate as they scroll into view. Each stat carries a number, an optional prefix and suffix, and a label, for example <em>5,000+ Happy clients</em>.</p>
            <p>Set <strong>Columns</strong> for how many stats sit in a row, and switch the count-up animation off if you prefer a static number.</p>
            <h2 id="trust-badges">Trust Badges</h2>
            <p>A row of badges, each with an icon, a title and a subtitle. The <strong>Badges</strong> repeater holds one slot per badge, shown one at a time behind a row of chips, with an <strong>Add New Badge</strong> button.</p>
            <p>Three Style panels cover the row: <strong>Badge Box</strong> for padding, corner radius and shadow, <strong>Badge Icon</strong> for icon size, position and gap, and <strong>Badge Text</strong> for the title and subtitle typography.</p>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>The <strong>Icon</strong> panel follows the same repeater, so each badge has its own icon slot. Before 1.0.4 the editor preview drew the same shield for every badge while the published page drew the right one.</p></div>
            <h2 id="trust-logos">Client Logos</h2>
            <p>A trusted-by grid. Add each logo to the <strong>Logos</strong> repeater, then set:</p>
            <p>Client Logos controls.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Columns</code></td><td>number</td><td>4 / 3 / 2</td><td>Logos per row on desktop, tablet and mobile.</td></tr><tr><td><code>logoHeight</code></td><td>number</td><td>60</td><td>Logo height in pixels. Widths follow each image.</td></tr><tr><td><code>grayscale</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>Shows logos in grey and restores their colour on hover.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Logo Tile Background</code></td><td>colour</td><td>Transparent</td><td>The tile behind each logo, in the Colors panel.</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Give every logo the same visual weight before uploading. Greyscale hides colour differences but not a logo that is twice the size of its neighbours.</p></div>
            <h2 id="trust-bubbles">Floating Avatar Bubbles</h2>
            <p>Photo bubbles that reveal a quote when a visitor points at one. It is a compact way to show ten reviews in the space of a headline.</p>
            <p>The <strong>Colors</strong> panel names its own parts: <strong>Bubble Background</strong> is the tooltip behind the quote, and <strong>Accent</strong> is the ring around each photo.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="media-blocks" data-group="Block Library" data-title="Video, Audio and Before / After">
            <h1>Video, Audio and Before / After</h1>
            <p class="lead">The three blocks that carry media. A recorded customer is more convincing than a typed quote, and these are the blocks that show one.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="video" data-src="" data-caption="Video walkthrough - adding a YouTube URL to Video Testimonials, an MP3 to Audio Testimonials, and two images to the Before / After slider, then testing all three on the front end"></figure>
            <h2 id="media-video">Video Testimonials</h2>
            <p>Video reviews shown as thumbnail cards that open in a lightbox on click. Paste a YouTube link, a Vimeo link or a direct MP4 URL into each card.</p>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Insert the block and open the card editor on the <strong>General</strong> tab.</li><li>Paste the video URL into the card.</li><li>Add the reviewer’s <strong>Name</strong> and <strong>Designation</strong>.</li><li>Set <strong>Columns</strong> for how many videos sit in a row. The default is 3.</li></ol>
            <p>On the Style tab, the <strong>Colors</strong> panel calls its surface role <strong>Play Button</strong>, because that round button is the only thing the colour paints on this block.</p>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>Videos load only when a visitor clicks one, so a page of video reviews stays fast.</p></div>
            <h2 id="media-audio">Audio Testimonials</h2>
            <p>Voice note reviews with a waveform style player. Each card gains an <strong>Audio File</strong> field next to the usual review fields, so one card carries a photo, a name, a rating and a clip.</p>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Keep clips under a minute and name the file after the customer. A short clip gets listened to, and a clear filename saves you guessing in the media library later.</p></div>
            <h2 id="media-compare">Before & After</h2>
            <p>Two images and a handle to drag between them. Use it for results your customers can see, such as a redesign or a repair.</p>
            <p>Before / After controls.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Before image / After image</code></td><td>image</td><td>None</td><td>The two images. The slider appears as soon as one is set, so add both for a comparison that makes sense.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Before label / After label</code></td><td>text</td><td>Before / After</td><td>The corner captions.</td></tr><tr><td><code>startPosition</code></td><td>number</td><td>50</td><td>Where the handle sits on load, as a percentage.</td></tr><tr><td><code>orientation</code></td><td>select</td><td>Horizontal</td><td>Drag the divider left and right, or up and down.</td></tr><tr><td><code>interaction</code></td><td>select</td><td>Drag</td><td>Whether the divider follows a drag or a hover.</td></tr><tr><td><code>aspectRatio</code></td><td>select</td><td>Auto</td><td>Fixes the frame shape, or follows the images.</td></tr><tr><td><code>showLabels</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>Shows the two corner captions.</td></tr><tr><td><code>labelPosition</code></td><td>select</td><td>Bottom</td><td>Which edge the captions sit on.</td></tr><tr><td><code>gripSize</code></td><td>number</td><td>38</td><td>The drag handle diameter, in pixels.</td></tr><tr><td><code>dividerWidth</code></td><td>number</td><td>3</td><td>The divider line thickness, in pixels.</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>The <strong>Colors</strong> panel styles the parts that used to be fixed: <strong>Label Background</strong>, <strong>Label Text</strong> and <strong>Handle Arrow</strong>.</p>
            <div class="callout warn"><span class="co-label">Important</span><p>Use two images of the same size, shot from the same angle. Different crops make the slider look broken rather than persuasive.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="testimonial-form-block" data-group="Forms & Polls" data-title="Testimonial Form Block">
            <h1>Testimonial Form Block</h1>
            <p class="lead">The Testimonial Form block lets your visitors write their own review. Every submission is saved as pending, so nothing appears on your site without your approval.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="video" data-src="" data-caption="Video walkthrough - placing the Testimonial Form block, turning the Email and Company fields on, renaming the labels and button, and submitting a test review"></figure>
            <h2 id="form-place">Place the Form</h2>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Insert the <strong>Testimonials</strong> block on the page where you want the form.</li><li>Choose <strong>Testimonial Form</strong> from the layout picker, or search for it in the block switcher.</li><li>Set the <strong>Form Title</strong> and <strong>Submit Button Text</strong> in the <strong>Form Settings</strong> panel.</li><li>Publish the page.</li></ol>
            <p>A logged-out visitor can use the form. That is the point, so put it on a public page such as a reviews page or the foot of a product page.</p>
            <h2 id="form-fields">Choose the Fields</h2>
            <p>Name and Review are always asked for, because a submission without them is rejected. The rest are toggles:</p>
            <p>The optional fields, and whether they are on to begin with.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Rating</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>A star rating from 1 to 5.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Designation</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>The line under the name.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Email</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>Off</td><td>Stored with the submission and never shown publicly.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Company</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>Off</td><td>Stored with the submission.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Photo</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>Off</td><td>A JPG, PNG, GIF or WebP upload, used as the review photo.</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>You can rewrite every field label and placeholder, so the form can speak in your own words rather than the defaults.</p>
            <h2 id="form-messages">The Three Messages</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Message</th><th>When it shows</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Form Title</strong></td><td>Above the form. The default is <em>Share your experience</em>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Submit Button Text</strong></td><td>On the button. The default is <em>Submit testimonial</em>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Success message</strong></td><td>After a successful submission. The default says the review is awaiting review.</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Say in the success message that you read submissions before publishing them. It sets the expectation and stops people submitting twice.</p></div>
            <h2 id="form-style">Style the Form</h2>
            <p>Two Style panels cover the fields:</p>
            <ul><li><strong>Field Labels</strong>, with <strong>Typography</strong> and <strong>Color</strong>.</li><li><strong>Input Fields</strong>, with <strong>Typography</strong>, <strong>Text Color</strong>, <strong>Placeholder Color</strong>, <strong>Background</strong>, <strong>Border Color</strong>, <strong>Focus Border Color</strong>, <strong>Border Width</strong>, <strong>Corner Radius</strong>, <strong>Review Box Height</strong> and <strong>Padding</strong>.</li></ul>
            <p>The <strong>Colors</strong> panel adds the four result colours: <strong>Thank-you Message</strong>, <strong>Thank-you Background</strong>, <strong>Error Message</strong> and <strong>Error Background</strong>.</p>
            <p>The submit button has its own controls for background, hover background, text colour, hover text colour, typography, padding, radius, width and alignment.</p>
            <h2 id="form-submit">What Happens on Submit</h2>
            <ol class="steps"><li>The form posts to the plugin’s own REST route with a security token.</li><li>A new testimonial is created with the status <strong>pending</strong>.</li><li>The rating, designation, company and email are stored as meta, and any photo becomes the featured image.</li><li>The submission appears under <strong>Testimonials &rarr; Submissions</strong>, and the submenu shows a count. See <a href="#submissions" data-nav>Reviewing Customer Submissions</a>.</li></ol>
            <div class="callout warn"><span class="co-label">Important</span><p>A visitor who submits with the name or review empty gets an error, not a saved draft. Keep both fields obviously required in your labels.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="nps-poll-block" data-group="Forms & Polls" data-title="Feedback & NPS Poll Block">
            <h1>Feedback & NPS Poll Block</h1>
            <p class="lead">The poll asks one question on a numbered scale and stores the answers. It is the standard Net Promoter Score question, ready to place on a page.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="video" data-src="" data-caption="Video walkthrough - setting up the Feedback &amp; NPS Poll block with a question, a 0 to 10 scale and its two end labels, voting once on the front end, and reading the result on the admin screen"></figure>
            <h2 id="poll-setup">Set Up the Question</h2>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Insert the <strong>Testimonials</strong> block and choose <strong>Feedback &amp; NPS Poll</strong>.</li><li>Open the <strong>Poll Settings</strong> panel.</li><li>Type the <strong>Poll Question</strong>, for example <em>How likely are you to recommend us?</em></li><li>Add a <strong>Poll Subtitle</strong> if the question needs context.</li><li>Set <strong>Minimum Mark</strong> and <strong>Maximum Mark</strong>, and the two scale labels.</li></ol>
            <p>The scale controls.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Minimum Mark</code></td><td>number</td><td>0</td><td>The lowest number a visitor can pick.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Maximum Mark</code></td><td>number</td><td>10</td><td>The highest number a visitor can pick.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Low Scale Label</code></td><td>text</td><td>Not likely</td><td>The caption under the lowest number.</td></tr><tr><td><code>High Scale Label</code></td><td>text</td><td>Very likely</td><td>The caption under the highest number.</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>Keep the standard 0 to 10 scale if you want your score to be comparable with anyone else’s Net Promoter Score.</p></div>
            <h2 id="poll-style">Style the Poll</h2>
            <p>The <strong>Poll Style</strong> panel covers everything the poll draws:</p>
            <ul><li><strong>Poll Text</strong>: typography for the title, description and scale labels.</li><li><strong>Scale Buttons</strong>: typography, <strong>Button Size (px)</strong>, <strong>Button Gap (px)</strong>, corner radius, shadow, and the two number colours.</li><li><strong>Poll Box</strong>: padding, corner radius and shadow for the box behind the poll.</li></ul>
            <p>The <strong>Colors</strong> panel names the poll’s two surfaces for what they paint: <strong>Poll Box Background</strong> and <strong>Number Button Background</strong>.</p>
            <h2 id="poll-results">Read the Results</h2>
            <p>Every vote is stored on your site and summarised under <strong>Testimonials &rarr; Feedback &amp; NPS Poll</strong>, with your score and a breakdown by band. See <a href="#nps-dashboard" data-nav>Feedback &amp; NPS Poll Results</a>.</p>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>One poll per page, and place it after the visitor has had the experience you are asking about. A poll at the top of a landing page collects noise.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="interactive-blocks" data-group="Forms & Polls" data-title="Accordion, Table and Toast">
            <h1>Accordion, Table and Toast</h1>
            <p class="lead">Three blocks that hold more content than they show, so a long page stays short.</p>
            <h2 id="inter-faq">FAQ Review Accordion</h2>
            <p>Collapsible rows, each with a question and an answer. Use the <strong>Accordion Rows</strong> panel to add rows, with <strong>Question</strong> and <strong>Answer</strong> per row and an <strong>Add New Row</strong> button.</p>
            <p>The Accordion panel.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>One at a time</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>Off</td><td>Closes the open row when another is opened.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Open first row</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>Off</td><td>Shows the first answer already expanded.</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>The <strong>Accordion Rows</strong> style panel sets <strong>Corner Radius</strong>, <strong>Row Background</strong>, <strong>Shadow</strong>, <strong>Row Gap</strong>, <strong>Question Padding</strong> and <strong>Answer Padding</strong>.</p>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Turn on <strong>Open first row</strong> so visitors can see what the rows contain. A wall of closed rows often goes unclicked.</p></div>
            <h2 id="inter-table">Comparison Review Table</h2>
            <p>A side-by-side table of customers, their ratings and their reviews. Set <strong>Table Title</strong> for the heading and rename the three column headers, which default to <strong>Customer</strong>, <strong>Rating</strong> and <strong>Review</strong>.</p>
            <p>On the Style tab the table has its own colour roles: <strong>Header Row</strong> for the top strip, and separate <strong>Divider Color</strong> and <strong>Divider Width</strong> for the rules between rows.</p>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>Tables scroll sideways on a phone rather than squeezing. Keep the review column short so the table stays readable at that width.</p></div>
            <h2 id="inter-toast">Social Proof Toast</h2>
            <p>A small notification that rotates through recent reviews, the way a booking site shows recent activity. It ships with three notifications so the rotation is visible the moment you insert it.</p>
            <p>The <strong>Toast Settings</strong> panel takes a <strong>Toast Message</strong> and a <strong>Time Label</strong> such as <em>Just now</em> or <em>2 min ago</em>. The <strong>Notification</strong> panel controls the motion:</p>
            <p>The Notification panel.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Rotation Interval (seconds)</code></td><td>number</td><td>4</td><td>How long each notification stays up before the next one. 1 to 60.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Pause on hover</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>Off</td><td>Holds the current notification while the pointer is on it.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Pause while editing</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>Off</td><td>Stops the rotation in the editor only.</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>The toast is narrower than its column, so use <strong>Alignment</strong> in the <strong>Width &amp; Height</strong> panel to place it left, centre or right.</p>
            <div class="callout warn"><span class="co-label">Important</span><p>Write time labels that stay true. <em>2 min ago</em> is fixed text, not a live clock, so it will still say two minutes tomorrow.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="general-tab" data-group="Content Settings" data-title="The General Tab">
            <h1>The General Tab</h1>
            <p class="lead">The block sidebar has two tabs. General holds what the block shows; Style holds how it looks. This page is the map of General.</p>
            <h2 id="gen-panels">The Panels</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Panel</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Select / Switch Block</strong></td><td>Opens the layout switcher. See <a href="#block-switcher" data-nav>Switching Layouts</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Content Source</strong></td><td>Manual cards or the Testimonials post type. See <a href="#content-source" data-nav>Content Source</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Add New Item</strong></td><td>The review cards. See <a href="#review-cards" data-nav>Adding and Editing Review Cards</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Block-specific settings</strong></td><td>One panel named after the block, for single-widget blocks such as the badges, the form and the poll.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Elements</strong></td><td>Which card parts show. See <a href="#elements-excerpt" data-nav>Elements, Excerpt and Expand</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excerpt &amp; Expand</strong></td><td>Cuts long reviews and adds a button to reveal the rest.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Layout</strong></td><td>Arrangement, theme, columns and gaps. See <a href="#layout-arrangement" data-nav>Layout, Arrangement and Themes</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Slider</strong> / <strong>Marquee</strong> / <strong>Autoplay</strong> / <strong>Notification</strong> / <strong>Accordion</strong></td><td>Motion, for the layouts that move. See <a href="#slider-settings" data-nav>Slider and Marquee Settings</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Icon</strong></td><td>The icon or artwork a block draws, where it has one.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="gen-conditional">Why Panels Come and Go</h2>
            <p>The sidebar only offers controls that change something on the layout you picked. A control that could not move a pixel is hidden rather than shown doing nothing.</p>
            <ul><li>A <strong>timeline</strong> reads top to bottom, so it offers no column count.</li><li>A <strong>video block</strong> has no review paragraph, so it offers no review typography.</li><li>A <strong>badge</strong> draws no card list, so it offers no Elements toggles.</li><li>The <strong>card stack</strong> hides its dot and arrow colours until a second review exists to move between.</li></ul>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>If a control you expected is missing, the layout does not paint that part. Switch layout and it comes back.</p></div>
            <h2 id="gen-toolbar">The Toolbar</h2>
            <p>The block toolbar above the block holds the alignment control, including <strong>Wide width</strong> and <strong>Full width</strong>. Hero and marquee layouts are designed for full width.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="elements-excerpt" data-group="Content Settings" data-title="Elements, Excerpt and Expand">
            <h1>Elements, Excerpt and Expand</h1>
            <p class="lead">A review card has five parts and you decide which of them appear. Long reviews can be cut to a set length with a button to read the rest.</p>
            <h2 id="el-toggles">The Elements Toggles</h2>
            <p>The Elements panel. Every toggle applies to the whole block.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Image</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>The reviewer’s photo.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Name</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>The reviewer’s name.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Designation</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>The line under the name.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Review Text</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>The review itself.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Rating</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>The star row.</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>Switching a part off removes it from the markup, so it takes no space and leaves no gap.</p>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>No photos of your customers? Switch <strong>Image</strong> off across the block rather than leaving empty circles. The layouts close up neatly without it.</p></div>
            <h2 id="el-excerpt">Cut Long Reviews</h2>
            <p>The <strong>Excerpt &amp; Expand</strong> panel keeps a grid tidy when your reviews are different lengths.</p>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Turn on <strong>Expand / Less button</strong>.</li><li>Set <strong>Excerpt length</strong>, the number of characters shown before the cut. The default is 120, and the range is 10 to 1000.</li><li>Set the <strong>Expand Text</strong> and <strong>Collapse Text</strong> button labels. They default to <em>Expand</em> and <em>Less</em>.</li></ol>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>Excerpt length only does something while the button is on. With the button off, the whole review is printed however long it is.</p></div>
            <h2 id="el-button">Style the Button</h2>
            <p>With the button on, the Style tab gains an <strong>Expand / Less Button</strong> panel with <strong>Typography</strong>, <strong>Color</strong> and <strong>Hover Color</strong>.</p>
            <h2 id="el-none">Layouts Without Review Text</h2>
            <p>Blocks that print no review paragraph, such as the badges and Client Logos, have neither panel. There is nothing to trim and no card parts to toggle.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="layout-arrangement" data-group="Content Settings" data-title="Layout, Arrangement and Themes">
            <h1>Layout, Arrangement and Themes</h1>
            <p class="lead">One set of reviews can be laid out seven ways and styled with six card themes, all without switching block.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="video" data-src="" data-caption="Video walkthrough - moving one set of reviews through Default, Slider, Masonry, List and Marquee arrangements, then through the six card themes"></figure>
            <h2 id="la-arrangement">Arrangement</h2>
            <p>The <strong>Arrangement</strong> control in the <strong>Layout</strong> panel decides how the cards are placed:</p>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Arrangement</th><th>Result</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Default</strong></td><td>A grid, using the column counts you set.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Slider</strong></td><td>A carousel with arrows and dots.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Masonry</strong></td><td>A staggered grid that suits cards of different heights.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>List</strong></td><td>One review per full-width row.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Marquee</strong></td><td>A strip scrolling continuously in one direction.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3D Slider</strong></td><td>A carousel whose cards flip on a perspective axis.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Coverflow</strong></td><td>A carousel that centres one card and pushes its neighbours back.</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>Choosing an arrangement changes which motion panel appears, so the controls always match what the block is doing.</p>
            <h2 id="la-theme">Themes</h2>
            <p>The <strong>Theme</strong> control restyles the card itself: <strong>Default</strong>, then <strong>Theme-1</strong> through <strong>Theme-6</strong>. Each one rearranges the photo, name, designation, stars and review inside the card.</p>
            <p>Themes work on any layout built from the shared card, which is most of the grid, list and slider blocks. Bespoke layouts such as the badges and the timeline draw their own markup and offer no theme.</p>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Set the theme before you spend time on typography. A theme changes the order and size of the card parts, which is most of the visual difference.</p></div>
            <h2 id="la-columns">Columns and Gaps</h2>
            <p>The Layout panel’s spacing controls. All three are stored per device.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Columns</code></td><td>number</td><td>3 / 2 / 1</td><td>Cards per row on desktop, tablet and mobile.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Column Gap</code></td><td>length</td><td>30px</td><td>The space between columns.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Row Gap</code></td><td>length</td><td>40px</td><td>The space between rows.</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>Use the device icon next to <strong>Columns</strong> to switch between desktop, tablet and mobile. It follows the editor’s own preview device, so the control and the canvas always agree.</p>
            <h2 id="la-order">Card Order</h2>
            <p>Cards render in the order they appear in the card editor, so reorder them there. With <strong>Content Source</strong> set to <strong>Testimonials (CPT)</strong>, use <strong>Order By</strong> and <strong>Order</strong> instead.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="slider-settings" data-group="Content Settings" data-title="Slider and Marquee Settings">
            <h1>Slider and Marquee Settings</h1>
            <p class="lead">Once a layout moves, it gets its own panel. Sliders, coverflow carousels and marquees each have controls for speed, direction and how much a visitor can steer.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="video" data-src="" data-caption="Video walkthrough - turning on AutoPlay and Loop, styling the navigation arrows, and tuning 3D Depth, Card Spread and Card Width on the Coverflow Carousel"></figure>
            <h2 id="ss-slider">The Slider Panel</h2>
            <p>Shown for the Slider, 3D Slider and Coverflow arrangements.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>AutoPlay</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>Advances the slider on its own.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Slide Every (seconds)</code></td><td>number</td><td>3</td><td>How long each slide is held, from 1 to 15. Shown when AutoPlay is on.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Pause while editing</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>Off</td><td>Holds the slider still in the editor only. Shown when AutoPlay is on.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Loop</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>Runs back to the first slide after the last.</td></tr><tr><td><code>MouseWheel</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>Lets the wheel move the slider.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Navigation</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>Shows the previous and next arrows.</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout warn"><span class="co-label">Important</span><p><strong>MouseWheel</strong> takes the wheel over from the page while the pointer is on the slider. On a long page, switching it off is usually kinder.</p></div>
            <h2 id="ss-arrows">Style the Arrows</h2>
            <p>With <strong>Navigation</strong> on, the panel adds arrow styling: <strong>Arrow Size</strong>, <strong>Arrow Color</strong>, its hover colour and background, <strong>Arrow Border</strong> and <strong>Arrow Shadow</strong>. Leave them untouched to keep the slider’s own defaults.</p>
            <h2 id="ss-3d">The 3D Controls</h2>
            <p>The Coverflow and 3D Slider arrangements add the depth controls:</p>
            <p>Shown for Coverflow and 3D Slider only.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Card Width</code></td><td>length</td><td>Follows the layout</td><td>How wide the centre card is.</td></tr><tr><td><code>3D Depth</code></td><td>number</td><td>Layout default</td><td>How far back the side cards sit.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Card Spread</code></td><td>number</td><td>Layout default</td><td>How far apart the cards are pushed.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Slide shadows</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>Off</td><td>Draws the carousel’s own depth shadows.</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>These are left empty on purpose so each arrangement keeps the look it ships with. Setting one takes that arrangement off its default.</p></div>
            <h2 id="ss-marquee">The Marquee Panel</h2>
            <p>Shown for the Marquee arrangement.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Speed</code></td><td>number</td><td>30</td><td>How fast the strip scrolls. A lower number is slower.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Direction</code></td><td>select</td><td>Left</td><td>Left or Right.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Pause on hover</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>Stops the strip while the pointer is on it.</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Leave <strong>Pause on hover</strong> on. A marquee your visitor cannot stop is a marquee they cannot read.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="style-tab" data-group="Styling" data-title="The Style Tab">
            <h1>The Style Tab</h1>
            <p class="lead">The Style tab holds every appearance control. This page is the map, so you know which panel owns which pixel.</p>
            <h2 id="st-panels">The Panels</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Panel</th><th>What it paints</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Colors</strong></td><td>The layout’s own parts, named for what they are. See <a href="#colors-panel" data-nav>The Colors Panel</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Card</strong></td><td>The card back, padding, border, shadow and hover state. See <a href="#card-styling" data-nav>Card Styling</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Header Strip</strong></td><td>The tinted strip behind the photo and name, on the layouts that draw one.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Image</strong></td><td>Photo size, border and avatar ring. See <a href="#image-avatar" data-nav>Photos, Avatars and Icons</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Name</strong> / <strong>Designation</strong> / <strong>Review Text</strong></td><td>Fonts and colours. See <a href="#typography" data-nav>Typography</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Expand / Less Button</strong></td><td>The read-more button, when the excerpt button is on.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Width &amp; Height</strong></td><td>Block width, card height and alignment. See <a href="#size-spacing" data-nav>Size, Spacing and Alignment</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Spacing</strong></td><td>Block margin and card margin.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Block-specific panels</strong></td><td>Speech Bubble, Gradient Border, Popup, Poll Style, Badge Score, Badge Logo, Trust Badge and form field panels.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="st-one">One Control Per Pixel</h2>
            <p>Where two panels used to set the same value, one of them now owns it. On layouts that draw a card, <strong>Background Color</strong> and <strong>Border</strong> live in the <strong>Card</strong> panel and are dropped from <strong>Colors</strong>.</p>
            <p>Blocks with no card, such as the video block and the before/after slider, keep those colours in the <strong>Colors</strong> panel, since there is no Card panel to hold them.</p>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>Border style, side and corner radius have no palette role at all. They only ever live in the Card panel.</p></div>
            <h2 id="st-defaults">Where Nothing Is Set</h2>
            <p>Most colour controls start empty. An empty control means the layout keeps its own built-in value, which is why an untouched block looks finished.</p>
            <p>Clearing a control puts that part back to the layout default. It does not make it transparent.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="card-styling" data-group="Styling" data-title="Card Styling">
            <h1>Card Styling</h1>
            <p class="lead">The Card panel paints the box every review sits in: its background, padding, border, shadow and what happens on hover.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="video" data-src="" data-caption="Video walkthrough - styling one card from the Card panel: background, corner wash, padding, border, shadow, and the hover background and lift"></figure>
            <h2 id="cs-panel">The Card Panel</h2>
            <p>The Card panel, on the Style tab.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Background Color</code></td><td>colour</td><td>Transparent</td><td>The card back. Named for what it paints on layouts where that is not a flat card, for example Card Gradient Start on the quote box.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Corner wash</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>A soft tint in one corner of the card.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Wash Strength (%)</code></td><td>number</td><td>100</td><td>How strong the corner tint is. Shown when the wash is on.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Wash Color</code></td><td>colour</td><td>Follows the accent</td><td>The colour of the corner tint.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Padding</code></td><td>box</td><td>10px 15px</td><td>Space inside the card, per side.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Border</code></td><td>border</td><td>1px solid #0575e6, radius 3px</td><td>Width, style, colour, side and corner radius.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Shadow</code></td><td>shadow</td><td>None</td><td>The card’s drop shadow.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="cs-hover">The Hover State</h2>
            <p>Four controls set what a card does when the pointer is on it:</p>
            <p>The hover controls, in the same panel.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Background Color (hover)</code></td><td>colour</td><td>No change</td><td>The card back on hover.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Border Color (hover)</code></td><td>colour</td><td>No change</td><td>The card border on hover.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Shadow (hover)</code></td><td>shadow</td><td>No change</td><td>A deeper shadow reads as lifting the card.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Lift (px)</code></td><td>number</td><td>None</td><td>How far the card rises on hover.</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>A 4px lift with a slightly deeper shadow is enough. Larger values make a grid feel unstable as the pointer crosses it.</p></div>
            <h2 id="cs-header">The Header Strip</h2>
            <p>Layouts that draw a tinted strip behind the photo and name get a <strong>Header Strip</strong> panel, with <strong>Background Color</strong> and <strong>Padding</strong>. Centered Cards Grid is the clearest example.</p>
            <h2 id="cs-size">Card Size and Margin</h2>
            <p>Card height lives in <strong>Width &amp; Height</strong> and card margin in <strong>Spacing</strong>, because both are stored per device. See <a href="#size-spacing" data-nav>Size, Spacing and Alignment</a>.</p>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>Fixing <strong>Card Height</strong> makes every card in a grid the same height, whatever the review length. Leave it empty to let each card size itself.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="image-avatar" data-group="Styling" data-title="Photos, Avatars and Icons">
            <h1>Photos, Avatars and Icons</h1>
            <p class="lead">The Image panel sizes and frames the reviewer’s photo. The Icon panel handles the artwork a block draws for itself.</p>
            <h2 id="im-panel">The Image Panel</h2>
            <p>The Image panel, on the Style tab.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Width</code></td><td>number</td><td>50</td><td>Photo width in pixels.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Height</code></td><td>number</td><td>50</td><td>Photo height in pixels.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Border</code></td><td>border</td><td>1px solid #0575e6, radius 50%</td><td>The photo frame. A 50% radius makes it a circle.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Outer ring</code></td><td>toggle</td><td>On</td><td>Draws a ring around the photo.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Ring Width (px)</code></td><td>number</td><td>3</td><td>How thick the ring is. Shown when the ring is on.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Ring Color</code></td><td>colour</td><td>Follows the accent</td><td>The ring colour.</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Upload square photos, or crop them square in the media library. A rectangular photo in a circular frame gets cropped where you did not choose.</p></div>
            <h2 id="im-layouts">Avatar Size by Layout</h2>
            <p>Some layouts size the photo themselves rather than through this panel, because the photo is the layout. Avatar Reviews List, Floating Avatar Bubbles and the Hero Spotlight all use their own <strong>Avatar Size (px)</strong> control.</p>
            <h2 id="im-icon">The Icon Panel</h2>
            <p>Blocks that draw artwork of their own get an <strong>Icon</strong> panel with <strong>Icon Color</strong>, <strong>Icon Stroke Color</strong> and <strong>Icon Size</strong>. You can also pick a different icon from the library where the block allows it.</p>
            <p><strong>Icon Size</strong> applies to the block’s own built-in artwork, not only to an icon you picked yourself.</p>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>The Google, Capterra, Facebook, Trustpilot and G2 marks are deliberately kept out of the icon controls. They are trademarks, and a recoloured logo stops working as a recognisable badge. Their size is set by <strong>Logo Size (px)</strong> in the Badge Logo panel.</p></div>
            <h2 id="im-quote">Quote Marks and Stars</h2>
            <p>The quote mark on the layouts that draw one has its own size control. The star row is set in the <strong>Review Text</strong> panel, with <strong>Rating Icon Color</strong> and <strong>Rating Icon Size (px)</strong>.</p>
            <p>Leave the star size empty to keep each layout’s own size. The layouts do not agree on one, so a value here resizes several of them at once.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="typography" data-group="Styling" data-title="Typography">
            <h1>Typography</h1>
            <p class="lead">Three panels cover the text on a card: the name, the line under it, and the review itself. Each takes a full typography control and a colour.</p>
            <h2 id="ty-panels">The Three Panels</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Panel</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Name</strong></td><td>The reviewer’s name</td><td>Open Sans, 16px desktop, weight 700, line height 140%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Designation</strong></td><td>The line under the name</td><td>Open Sans, 14px, weight 400, line height 150%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Review Text</strong></td><td>The review paragraph</td><td>16px desktop, weight 400, line height 170%</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>Each <strong>Typography</strong> control sets the font family, size, weight and line height, and the size is stored per device.</p>
            <h2 id="ty-colors">Colours</h2>
            <p>The colour defaults.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Name colour</code></td><td>colour</td><td>#000</td><td>The reviewer’s name.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Designation colour</code></td><td>colour</td><td>#7B7B7B</td><td>The line under the name.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Review text colour</code></td><td>colour</td><td>#000</td><td>The review paragraph.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Rating Icon Color</code></td><td>colour</td><td>#FF8C02</td><td>The stars.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="ty-badges">Renamed on the Badges</h2>
            <p>On a review badge these panels style a heading and a count rather than a person, so they are renamed to say so: <strong>Badge Title</strong> and <strong>Review Count</strong>. The verified buyer seal names the second one <strong>Description</strong>.</p>
            <h2 id="ty-divider">The Designation Divider</h2>
            <p>Layouts that draw a short rule under the designation offer <strong>Color</strong>, <strong>Thickness</strong> and <strong>Length</strong> for it in the same panel.</p>
            <h2 id="ty-expand">The Expand Button</h2>
            <p>With the excerpt button on, the <strong>Expand / Less Button</strong> panel gives it <strong>Typography</strong>, <strong>Color</strong> and <strong>Hover Color</strong> of its own.</p>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Set the review text line height a little higher than you would for body copy. Reviews are read in short bursts and the extra space helps.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="colors-panel" data-group="Styling" data-title="The Colors Panel">
            <h1>The Colors Panel</h1>
            <p class="lead">The Colors panel is one list of colour roles per layout. Each role is named for the part it paints on that layout, so the label always matches what moves.</p>
            <h2 id="cp-roles">How the Roles Work</h2>
            <p>The layouts share a small set of colour roles, and each stylesheet reads the ones it needs. A layout is only offered a role it actually paints with, so the panel is short and every control does something.</p>
            <p>The shared roles and their default labels.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Accent</code></td><td>colour</td><td>Layout default</td><td>The layout’s highlight colour.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Card Surface</code></td><td>colour</td><td>Layout default</td><td>The card or widget back.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Border Color / Border Width</code></td><td>colour / number</td><td>Layout default</td><td>Outlines. Separate from dividers.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Divider Color / Divider Width</code></td><td>colour / number</td><td>Layout default</td><td>Rules between rows and sections.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Headings</code></td><td>colour</td><td>Layout default</td><td>Titles inside the widget.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Body Text</code></td><td>colour</td><td>Layout default</td><td>Ordinary text.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Secondary Text</code></td><td>colour</td><td>Layout default</td><td>Subtitles and counts.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Rating Stars</code></td><td>colour</td><td>Layout default</td><td>Stars on the single-widget layouts.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Bars & Tracks</code></td><td>colour</td><td>Layout default</td><td>Bar tracks, tiles, table header rows and dots.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="cp-renamed">Roles Renamed Per Layout</h2>
            <p>A generic label would describe something the layout does not draw, so several layouts rename their roles:</p>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Layout</th><th>Renamed to</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Video Testimonials</strong></td><td>Play Button</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Client Logos</strong></td><td>Logo Tile Background</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Comparison Review Table</strong></td><td>Header Row</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Floating Avatar Bubbles</strong></td><td>Bubble Background</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Stacked Review Cards</strong></td><td>Nav Dots, and Nav Buttons &amp; Active Dot</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Quote Box Showcase</strong></td><td>Card Gradient Start, Card Gradient End, Left Bar</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Customer Journey Timeline</strong></td><td>Timeline Rail Start &amp; Dots, Timeline Rail End</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Feedback &amp; NPS Poll</strong></td><td>Poll Box Background, Number Button Background</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="cp-special">Form and Slider Roles</h2>
            <p>Two blocks add roles of their own:</p>
            <ul><li>The <strong>Testimonial Form</strong> adds <strong>Thank-you Message</strong>, <strong>Thank-you Background</strong>, <strong>Error Message</strong> and <strong>Error Background</strong>.</li><li>The <strong>Before / After</strong> slider adds <strong>Label Background</strong>, <strong>Label Text</strong> and <strong>Handle Arrow</strong>.</li></ul>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>Every role starts empty and the stylesheet keeps its own value until you set one, so an untouched block renders exactly as designed.</p></div>
            <h2 id="cp-conditional">Roles That Wait for Content</h2>
            <p>A role whose element only exists in some states is hidden until that state is reached. The card stack’s dots and arrows need a second review, and the before/after label and handle colours need at least one of its two images picked.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="size-spacing" data-group="Styling" data-title="Size, Spacing and Alignment">
            <h1>Size, Spacing and Alignment</h1>
            <p class="lead">Two panels hold the measurements: Width & Height for the block and its cards, Spacing for the margins around them. All of it is stored per device.</p>
            <h2 id="sz-size">Width & Height</h2>
            <p>The Width & Height panel.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Block Width</code></td><td>length</td><td>Full column</td><td>How wide the whole block is. Accepts px, % and em.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Card Height</code></td><td>length</td><td>Follows content</td><td>Fixes every card to one height.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Alignment</code></td><td>select</td><td>Left</td><td>Left, Center or Right, for widgets narrower than their column.</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>The <strong>Alignment</strong> control is offered on the seven review badges and the Social Proof Toast, which shrink to fit rather than filling the column. Every other layout fills its column, where the control would change nothing.</p>
            <h2 id="sz-spacing">Spacing</h2>
            <p>The Spacing panel.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Block Margin</code></td><td>box</td><td>None</td><td>Space around the whole block, per side.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Card Margin</code></td><td>box</td><td>None</td><td>Space around each card, per side.</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>Space <em>inside</em> a card is <strong>Padding</strong> in the <strong>Card</strong> panel. Space <em>between</em> cards in a grid is <strong>Column Gap</strong> and <strong>Row Gap</strong> in the <strong>Layout</strong> panel.</p>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Use the grid gaps rather than card margins for a grid. Gaps keep the columns even; margins add up and can push the last column out of line.</p></div>
            <h2 id="sz-align">Block Alignment</h2>
            <p>The block toolbar’s own <strong>Wide width</strong> and <strong>Full width</strong> options are separate from this panel and depend on your theme supporting them. Hero and marquee layouts are designed for full width.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="responsive" data-group="Styling" data-title="Desktop, Tablet and Mobile">
            <h1>Desktop, Tablet and Mobile</h1>
            <p class="lead">Columns, gaps, font sizes, widths and margins are all stored three times over, once per device. Set them once per device and the block adapts on its own.</p>
            <h2 id="rs-switcher">The Device Switcher</h2>
            <p>Panels with per-device values show a small device icon. It follows the editor’s own preview device, so the control and the canvas always show the same thing.</p>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Switch the editor preview to <strong>Tablet</strong> or <strong>Mobile</strong> from the top toolbar.</li><li>Open the panel you want to change. Its device icon now reads that device.</li><li>Set the value. It applies to that device only.</li><li>Switch back to <strong>Desktop</strong> to confirm nothing there moved.</li></ol>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>Earlier versions let a panel edit tablet while the canvas still showed desktop. The two are tied together now, so the control always edits what you are looking at.</p></div>
            <h2 id="rs-what">What Is Stored Per Device</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Control</th><th>Panel</th><th>Default</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Columns</strong></td><td>Layout</td><td>3 desktop, 2 tablet, 1 mobile</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Column Gap</strong> and <strong>Row Gap</strong></td><td>Layout</td><td>30px and 40px</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Block Width</strong> and <strong>Card Height</strong></td><td>Width &amp; Height</td><td>Empty</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Block Margin</strong> and <strong>Card Margin</strong></td><td>Spacing</td><td>Empty</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Font sizes</strong></td><td>Name, Designation, Review Text</td><td>16 / 14 / 16px desktop</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="rs-recipe">A Sensible Starting Point</h2>
            <ul><li><strong>Desktop</strong>: three columns for short reviews, two for long ones.</li><li><strong>Tablet</strong>: two columns, and reduce the column gap.</li><li><strong>Mobile</strong>: one column, a smaller row gap, and one or two points off the review font size.</li></ul>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Check a slider or marquee on mobile before publishing. Both move sideways, and a card width that works on desktop can leave a phone showing half a card.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="dashboard" data-group="Admin Screens" data-title="Demo & Help Dashboard">
            <h1>Demo & Help Dashboard</h1>
            <p class="lead">Demo & Help is the plugin’s own dashboard: a getting-started guide, a live demo of every block, the block switches, and the settings.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="video" data-src="" data-caption="Video walkthrough - a tour of Testimonials &gt; Demo &amp; Help: the Welcome tabs, the Demos grid opening a live block preview, All Blocks, and Settings"></figure>
            <h2 id="db-open">Open It</h2>
            <p>Go to <strong>Testimonials &rarr; Demo &amp; Help</strong>. The plugin sends you here once, the first time it is activated.</p>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Page</th><th>What it holds</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Welcome</strong></td><td>Four step-by-step guides, one per way of using the blocks, and the changelog.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>All Blocks</strong></td><td>Every block with an on/off switch. See <a href="#all-blocks" data-nav>Turning Blocks On and Off</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Demos</strong></td><td>A live preview of any block, rendered on your own site with your own theme.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Settings</strong></td><td>The uninstall data toggle. See <a href="#settings-uninstall" data-nav>Settings and Uninstall Data</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Our Plugins</strong></td><td>The rest of the bPlugins range.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="db-welcome">The Welcome Guides</h2>
            <p>Welcome carries four tabs, each a numbered walkthrough:</p>
            <ul><li><strong>Gutenberg</strong>: insert the block, pick a layout, choose the content source, publish.</li><li><strong>ShortCode</strong>: write your reviews in the post type first, then point a block at them.</li><li><strong>Elementor</strong>: write the reviews, then embed the block with Elementor’s own widget.</li><li><strong>Theme / PHP</strong>: query the post type in a template and render it yourself.</li></ul>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>The <strong>ShortCode</strong> tab is about the Testimonials post type, not a shortcode. This plugin registers no shortcode. See <a href="#usage-elsewhere" data-nav>Using the Blocks Outside the Block Editor</a>.</p></div>
            <h2 id="db-demos">The Demos</h2>
            <p>The <strong>Demos</strong> grid opens each block as a real preview on your own site, so you see it in your theme with your fonts rather than in a marketing screenshot.</p>
            <p>A block you have switched off has no demo link, so nothing offers a preview that would fail.</p>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Browse the demos before you build. It is faster than inserting forty blocks one at a time, and every preview is the real block.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="all-blocks" data-group="Admin Screens" data-title="Turning Blocks On and Off">
            <h1>Turning Blocks On and Off</h1>
            <p class="lead">Thirty-nine layouts is a long inserter list. The All Blocks screen switches off the ones your site does not use, so your editors see a shorter, clearer set.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="video" data-src="" data-caption="Video walkthrough - switching blocks off on the All Blocks screen, confirming they disappear from the inserter and the switcher modal, then switching them all back on"></figure>
            <h2 id="ab-switch">Switch Blocks Off</h2>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Go to <strong>Testimonials &rarr; Demo &amp; Help</strong> and open <strong>All Blocks</strong>.</li><li>Find the block, by group or with the search field.</li><li>Switch it off. The change saves on its own.</li><li>Use <strong>Activate All</strong> or <strong>Deactivate All</strong> to move the whole list at once.</li></ol>
            <p>The blocks are grouped the way you would look for them: <strong>Grids &amp; Lists</strong>, <strong>Masonry &amp; Stacks</strong>, <strong>Sliders &amp; Carousels</strong>, <strong>Marquee &amp; Toasts</strong>, <strong>Spotlight &amp; Story</strong>, <strong>Video &amp; Audio</strong>, <strong>Ratings &amp; Stats</strong>, <strong>Badges &amp; Trust</strong> and <strong>Feedback &amp; Forms</strong>.</p>
            <figure class="media-slot" data-media="image" data-src="img/docs/05-all-blocks-screen.png" data-caption="The All Blocks screen: search field, Activate All and Deactivate All buttons, group headings, and a row of blocks with their switches. 1200px wide, #0852c2 canvas, Screely frame, red #ef4444 arrow on one switch, TinyPNG"></figure>
            <h2 id="ab-effect">What Switching Off Does</h2>
            <ul><li>The layout leaves the block switcher modal and the layout picker.</li><li>Its demo link disappears from the <strong>Demos</strong> page.</li><li>It is no longer registered, so it cannot be inserted.</li></ul>
            <div class="callout warn"><span class="co-label">Important</span><p>A page that already uses a block you switch off will not render it. Switch the block back on, or replace it on that page, before you leave it off for good.</p></div>
            <h2 id="ab-parent">The One Block You Cannot Switch Off</h2>
            <p>The parent <strong>Testimonials</strong> block has no switch. Every layout is its child, so switching the parent off would take all thirty-nine with it and leave this screen unable to switch any of them back on.</p>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>New blocks in a future release arrive switched on. The setting records which blocks are off, not which are on, so an update never hides a new layout from you.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="nps-dashboard" data-group="Admin Screens" data-title="Feedback & NPS Poll Results">
            <h1>Feedback & NPS Poll Results</h1>
            <p class="lead">Every vote from a Feedback & NPS Poll block is stored on your own site and summarised on this screen. Nothing is sent anywhere else.</p>
            <h2 id="nps-read">Read the Screen</h2>
            <p>Go to <strong>Testimonials &rarr; Feedback &amp; NPS Poll</strong>. The submenu carries the total number of votes collected so far.</p>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Figure</th><th>What it means</th></tr><tr><td><strong>NPS score</strong></td><td>The share of promoters minus the share of detractors, as a whole number.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total votes</strong></td><td>How many people answered.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Band breakdown</strong></td><td>How many votes fell into each band, with a percentage.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Vote list</strong></td><td>Every individual vote, with the mark and its band.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="nps-bands">The Three Bands</h2>
            <p>The default bands on a 0 to 10 scale.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>Promoter</code></td><td>band</td><td>Marks 9 and above</td><td>Counted in full towards the score.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Passive</code></td><td>band</td><td>Marks 7 and 8</td><td>Counted in the total but not in the score.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Detractor</code></td><td>band</td><td>Marks below 7</td><td>Subtracted from the score.</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>You can move the two thresholds and rename the bands on this screen, then save. <strong>Reset</strong> puts the standard values back.</p>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>The passive floor is always kept below the promoter floor, so the middle band can never be squeezed out of existence.</p></div>
            <h2 id="nps-clear">Clear the Votes</h2>
            <ul><li>Delete a single vote from its row in the list.</li><li>Use <strong>Clear all</strong> to empty the whole record.</li></ul>
            <div class="callout warn"><span class="co-label">Important</span><p>Clearing votes cannot be undone, and the plugin keeps the most recent 5,000 votes. Note the score somewhere before you clear, if you want to compare later.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="settings-uninstall" data-group="Admin Screens" data-title="Settings and Uninstall Data">
            <h1>Settings and Uninstall Data</h1>
            <p class="lead">The Settings page holds one decision: whether deleting the plugin also deletes your reviews.</p>
            <h2 id="set-toggle">The Uninstall Toggle</h2>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Go to <strong>Testimonials &rarr; Demo &amp; Help</strong> and open <strong>Settings</strong>.</li><li>Set the delete-data-on-uninstall toggle the way you want it. It is off to begin with.</li><li>The change saves on its own and confirms which choice is now stored.</li></ol>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What happens when you delete the plugin</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Off</strong> (the default)</td><td>Your testimonials, submissions, poll votes and settings stay in the database.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>On</strong></td><td>All of it is removed: testimonials in every state, photos submitted through the form, poll votes and the plugin’s own settings.</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout warn"><span class="co-label">Important</span><p>With the toggle on, deleting the plugin also deletes photos your visitors uploaded, because they are attached to the testimonials. Take a database backup first.</p></div>
            <h2 id="set-deactivate">Deactivating Is Safe</h2>
            <p>Deactivating the plugin changes nothing. The clean-up only runs when the plugin is deleted, and only when you switched the toggle on.</p>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Leave the toggle off unless you are certain. It is the difference between reinstalling the plugin and finding your reviews still there, and finding them gone.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="usage-elsewhere" data-group="Reference & Help" data-title="Using the Blocks Elsewhere">
            <h1>Using the Blocks Elsewhere</h1>
            <p class="lead">The plugin registers blocks and nothing else. This page covers your options outside the block editor, and is honest about what is not possible.</p>
            <h2 id="ue-noshortcode">There Is No Shortcode</h2>
            <p>The plugin registers no shortcode. There is no <code>[testimonials]</code> tag, and pasting one prints the text as it stands.</p>
            <p>The reviews themselves live in the Testimonials post type, so anything that can query a post type can display them.</p>
            <h2 id="ue-elementor">Elementor</h2>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Write your reviews under <strong>Testimonials &rarr; Add New</strong> and publish them.</li><li>Edit the page in Elementor.</li><li>Drag in Elementor’s <strong>Gutenberg Block</strong> widget, then choose the <strong>Testimonials</strong> block inside it.</li><li>Set the layout and the content source as you would in the block editor, and set <strong>Content Source</strong> to <strong>Testimonials (CPT)</strong>.</li></ol>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>Elementor’s <strong>Shortcode</strong> widget cannot help here, since there is no shortcode to paste into it. Use the Gutenberg Block widget.</p></div>
            <h2 id="ue-fse">Block Themes and the Site Editor</h2>
            <p>The blocks work anywhere the block editor does, so you can place them in a template, a template part or a reusable pattern in the Site Editor. A testimonials footer built as a pattern only has to be built once.</p>
            <h2 id="ue-php">In a PHP Template</h2>
            <p>To render reviews yourself, query the post type and read the three meta fields:</p>
            <div class="codeblock"><pre><code>&lt;?php
$reviews = get_posts( [
    'post_type'      =&gt; 'testimonial',
    'post_status'    =&gt; 'publish',
    'posts_per_page' =&gt; 6,
] );

foreach ( $reviews as $review ) {
    $rating      = (float) get_post_meta( $review-&gt;ID, 'bpbtb_rating', true );
    $designation = get_post_meta( $review-&gt;ID, 'bpbtb_designation', true );
    $company     = get_post_meta( $review-&gt;ID, 'bpbtb_company', true );
    ?&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
        &lt;?php echo wp_kses_post( $review-&gt;post_content ); ?&gt;
        &lt;cite&gt;&lt;?php echo esc_html( get_the_title( $review ) ); ?&gt;&lt;/cite&gt;
        &lt;span&gt;&lt;?php echo esc_html( $designation ); ?&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;?php
}</code></pre></div>
            <p>The plugin’s own helper does the same job and returns the shape its blocks use. See <a href="#developer" data-nav>Developer Reference</a>.</p>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Rendering the block is almost always easier than rendering the reviews yourself. Reach for PHP only when you need markup the layouts do not offer.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="developer" data-group="Reference & Help" data-title="Developer Reference">
            <h1>Developer Reference</h1>
            <p class="lead">The post type, meta fields, REST routes, hooks, options and CSS custom properties the plugin registers, as of version 1.0.4.</p>
            <h2 id="dev-cpt">Post Type and Meta</h2>
            <p>The testimonial post type and its meta fields.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>testimonial</code></td><td>post type</td><td>None</td><td>Not public, shown in the admin, available over REST. Supports title, editor and thumbnail.</td></tr><tr><td><code>bpbtb_rating</code></td><td>number</td><td>5</td><td>The star rating, 0 to 5 in steps of 0.1. Registered as REST-visible meta.</td></tr><tr><td><code>bpbtb_designation</code></td><td>string</td><td>Empty</td><td>The line under the name.</td></tr><tr><td><code>bpbtb_company</code></td><td>string</td><td>Empty</td><td>The reviewer’s company.</td></tr><tr><td><code>bpbtb_email</code></td><td>string</td><td>Empty</td><td>Set by the form only. Never rendered on the front end.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="dev-rest">REST Routes</h2>
            <p>Both routes are POST only.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>bptmb/v1/submit</code></td><td>POST</td><td>Public</td><td>The Testimonial Form. Requires a name and a review, checks a nonce, and creates a pending testimonial.</td></tr><tr><td><code>bptmb/v1/submit-nps</code></td><td>POST</td><td>Public</td><td>One poll vote. Requires a mark inside the block’s scale.</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>The testimonial post type also answers <code>/wp-json/wp/v2/testimonial</code> for logged-in users with permission, which is how the blocks list your reviews in the editor.</p>
            <h2 id="dev-hooks">Hooks</h2>
            <p>One action and three filters.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>bpbtb_testimonial_submitted</code></td><td>action</td><td>None</td><td>Fires after a form submission is saved. Passes the new pending post ID.</td></tr><tr><td><code>bpbtb_demo_url</code></td><td>filter</td><td>The local preview URL</td><td>One block’s demo URL. Passes the URL and the preview slug.</td></tr><tr><td><code>bpbtb_demo_index_url</code></td><td>filter</td><td>The Demos screen</td><td>Where the whole demo collection is browsed.</td></tr><tr><td><code>bpbtb_nps_categories</code></td><td>filter</td><td>The stored bands</td><td>The NPS thresholds, labels and colours.</td></tr></table></div>
            <p>A worked example, emailing yourself whenever a review comes in:</p>
            <div class="codeblock"><pre><code>add_action( 'bpbtb_testimonial_submitted', function ( $post_id ) {
    wp_mail(
        get_option( 'admin_email' ),
        'New testimonial awaiting review',
        get_edit_post_link( $post_id, 'raw' )
    );
} );</code></pre></div>
            <h2 id="dev-helpers">Helper Functions</h2>
            <p>Both are available once the plugin is loaded.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>bpbtb_get_testimonial_items( $args )</code></td><td>function</td><td>None</td><td>Returns published testimonials in the block item shape. Takes number, orderBy and order.</td></tr><tr><td><code>bpbtb_prepare_block_items( $attributes )</code></td><td>function</td><td>None</td><td>Swaps in testimonials from the post type when a block’s data source is cpt.</td></tr><tr><td><code>bpbtb_demo_urls()</code></td><td>function</td><td>None</td><td>Every switched-on block’s demo URL, keyed by preview slug.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="dev-options">Options</h2>
            <p>What the plugin stores.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><code>bpbtb_disabled_blocks</code></td><td>array</td><td>Empty</td><td>Block names switched off on the All Blocks screen. Stored as the blocks that are off, so a new block arrives on.</td></tr><tr><td><code>bpbtb_delete_data_on_uninstall</code></td><td>boolean</td><td>false</td><td>Whether deleting the plugin removes the data.</td></tr><tr><td><code>bpbtb_nps_poll_votes</code></td><td>array</td><td>Empty</td><td>Poll votes, capped at the most recent 5,000.</td></tr><tr><td><code>bpbtb_nps_categories</code></td><td>array</td><td>Empty</td><td>Your own NPS thresholds, labels and colours.</td></tr></table></div>
            <h2 id="dev-blocks">Blocks and Namespace</h2>
            <p>All blocks use the <code>bptmb/</code> namespace and the <code>bplugins</code> editor category. <code>bptmb/b-testimonials</code> is the parent; the other thirty-nine name it as their <code>parent</code>, so they can only be inserted inside it.</p>
            <p>Demo previews are served on the front page with the query argument <code>bpbtb_demo=<slug></code>, and are limited to users who can edit posts.</p>
            <h2 id="dev-css">CSS Custom Properties</h2>
            <p>The Colors panel writes CSS custom properties, and every stylesheet rule keeps its own value as the fallback. You can set the same properties from your theme:</p>
            <div class="codeblock"><pre><code>.wp-block-bptmb-b-testimonials {
    --btb-accent: #0b81ee;
    --btb-surface: #ffffff;
    --btb-border: #e6e8f0;
    --btb-title: #101828;
    --btb-body: #344054;
    --btb-muted: #667085;
    --btb-star: #ff8c02;
    --btb-track: #f2f4f7;
}</code></pre></div>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table class="doc-table"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Paints</th></tr><tr><td><code>--btb-accent</code></td><td>The layout highlight</td></tr><tr><td><code>--btb-surface</code></td><td>Card or widget background</td></tr><tr><td><code>--btb-border</code> / <code>--btb-border-width</code></td><td>Outlines</td></tr><tr><td><code>--btb-divider</code> / <code>--btb-divider-width</code></td><td>Rules between rows</td></tr><tr><td><code>--btb-title</code>, <code>--btb-body</code>, <code>--btb-muted</code></td><td>Headings, body text, secondary text</td></tr><tr><td><code>--btb-star</code></td><td>Rating stars</td></tr><tr><td><code>--btb-track</code></td><td>Bar tracks, tiles, header rows and dots</td></tr><tr><td><code>--btb-success</code>, <code>--btb-success-bg</code></td><td>Form thank-you message and its background</td></tr><tr><td><code>--btb-error</code>, <code>--btb-error-bg</code></td><td>Form error message and its background</td></tr><tr><td><code>--btb-ba-label-bg</code>, <code>--btb-ba-label-color</code>, <code>--btb-ba-grip</code></td><td>Before / after labels and handle arrow</td></tr></table></div>
            <div class="callout"><span class="co-label">Note</span><p>Text domain: <code>b-testimonials-block</code>. Every string is translatable, and the plugin ships a POT file in its <code>languages</code> folder.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="troubleshooting" data-group="Reference & Help" data-title="Troubleshooting">
            <h1>Troubleshooting</h1>
            <p class="lead">The problems that come up most often, with the check that tells you which cause you have.</p>
            <h2 id="tb-missing">A Block Is Missing from the Inserter</h2>
            <p>Only one block appears in the inserter: <strong>Testimonials</strong>. That is by design, so check that first, then work through the rest.</p>
            <h3>You are looking for a layout, not the block</h3>
            <p>Layouts are not separate inserter entries. Insert <strong>Testimonials</strong> and pick the layout from the picker or the switcher. See <a href="#block-switcher" data-nav>Switching Layouts</a>.</p>
            <h3>The layout is switched off</h3>
            <p>Open <strong>Testimonials &rarr; Demo &amp; Help &rarr; All Blocks</strong> and switch it back on. See <a href="#all-blocks" data-nav>Turning Blocks On and Off</a>.</p>
            <h3>Even the parent block is missing</h3>
            <p>Check the plugin is active on <strong>Plugins</strong>, then reload the editor with a hard refresh so the editor assets are fetched again.</p>
            <h2 id="tb-samples">A Block Shows Sample Reviews on the Front End</h2>
            <p>Every layout ships with sample cards so the design is visible immediately. Seeing them published means the block is still holding them.</p>
            <ol class="steps"><li>Select the block and open the card editor on the <strong>General</strong> tab.</li><li>Replace or remove the sample cards.</li><li>Or set <strong>Content Source</strong> to <strong>Testimonials (CPT)</strong>, which ignores the cards entirely.</li><li>Update the page and clear your site cache.</li></ol>
            <h2 id="tb-cpt-empty">The CPT Source Shows Nothing</h2>
            <p>First check <strong>Testimonials &rarr; All Testimonials</strong> and count the published reviews. The cause follows from that.</p>
            <h3>The reviews are still pending</h3>
            <p>Only published testimonials are shown. Approve them under <strong>Testimonials &rarr; Submissions</strong>. See <a href="#submissions" data-nav>Reviewing Customer Submissions</a>.</p>
            <h3>Number is set too low</h3>
            <p>Raise <strong>Number</strong> in the <strong>Content Source</strong> panel. It defaults to 6.</p>
            <h3>Order By is set to Menu Order</h3>
            <p>Every testimonial sits at order zero, because the editor has no order field, so the sequence falls back to the date and can look arbitrary. Switch <strong>Order By</strong> to <strong>Date</strong> or <strong>Title</strong>.</p>
            <h2 id="tb-control">A Style Control Does Nothing</h2>
            <p>Check whether the control is in the panel that owns that pixel on this layout.</p>
            <h3>The layout does not paint that part</h3>
            <p>The sidebar hides controls a layout cannot use, so a control you can see should do something. If it does not, note the layout and tell support which panel it was in.</p>
            <h3>Your theme is more specific</h3>
            <p>Your theme can outrank the block’s own rules. Set the value from your theme with the block’s CSS custom properties instead. See <a href="#developer" data-nav>Developer Reference</a>.</p>
            <h3>A cached page is being served</h3>
            <p>Clear your page cache and any CSS or JS minification cache, then reload with a hard refresh.</p>
            <h2 id="tb-form">A Submission Never Arrives</h2>
            <p>Submit the form yourself and read the message it gives back. That message names the cause.</p>
            <h3>Please provide your name and a review</h3>
            <p>One of the two required fields was empty. Make both obviously required in your labels.</p>
            <h3>Security check failed</h3>
            <p>The page was served from cache long enough for its security token to expire. Reload the page and submit again, and exclude the page holding the form from your cache.</p>
            <h3>The photo did not arrive</h3>
            <p>Only JPG, PNG, GIF and WebP files are accepted, and the upload has to fit inside your server’s upload limit. Other formats are dropped and the rest of the submission is still saved.</p>
            <h2 id="tb-motion">A Slider or Marquee Does Not Move</h2>
            <ul><li>Check <strong>AutoPlay</strong> in the <strong>Slider</strong> panel, and <strong>Pause on hover</strong> in the <strong>Marquee</strong> panel.</li><li>Check whether <strong>Pause while editing</strong> is on. It only affects the editor, not the published page.</li><li>Look for JavaScript errors in your browser console. Another plugin can stop the script that drives the movement.</li><li>Confirm you have more reviews than fit on screen. A slider with one card has nowhere to slide.</li></ul>
            <h2 id="tb-badge">A Badge Shows the Wrong Score</h2>
            <p>Badge scores are typed in by hand and are never fetched from Google, Trustpilot, G2, Facebook or Capterra. Update the <strong>Rating Score</strong> and <strong>Review Count</strong> fields in the block’s own settings panel.</p>
            <div class="callout tip"><span class="co-label">Tip</span><p>Still stuck? Note the block name, the layout, your WordPress and plugin versions, and what you expected to happen, then post it on the <a href="https://wordpress.org/support/plugin/b-testimonials-block/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">support forum</a>.</p></div>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="faq" data-group="Reference & Help" data-title="Frequently Asked Questions">
            <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
            <p class="lead">Short answers to the questions that come up most.</p>
            <h2 id="faq-list">Questions and Answers</h2>
            <h3>Is the plugin free?</h3><p>Yes. This version has no Pro tier, no licence key and no locked features. All thirty-nine layouts, the post type, the form, the submissions screen and the poll are included.</p><h3>Does it pull reviews from Google or Trustpilot?</h3><p>No. The badge blocks are styled to look like those services, and you type the score and review count yourself. Nothing is fetched from any review site.</p><h3>Is there a shortcode?</h3><p>No. The plugin registers blocks only. To use it in Elementor, use Elementor’s Gutenberg Block widget; in a theme template, query the testimonial post type in PHP.</p><h3>Can visitors submit their own reviews?</h3><p>Yes. Place the Testimonial Form block on a public page. Every submission is saved as pending and appears under Testimonials, Submissions for you to approve, reject or delete.</p><h3>Can I use the same reviews on several pages?</h3><p>Yes. Write them under Testimonials, Add New, then set Content Source to Testimonials (CPT) in each block. Editing a review updates every block showing it.</p><h3>Will I lose my reviews if I change layout?</h3><p>No. Your cards, photos and ratings are shared by every layout, so switching keeps them. Only settings the new layout has no panel for stop applying, and they are kept in case you switch back.</p><h3>Does it work with my theme?</h3><p>Yes. The blocks are responsive and work with classic themes and block themes. If your theme overrides a colour, set it with the block’s CSS custom properties from your theme.</p><h3>Can I hide the reviewer photos?</h3><p>Yes. Switch Image off in the Elements panel. The layouts close up neatly rather than leaving empty circles.</p><h3>How do I show fewer blocks to my editors?</h3><p>Switch the ones you do not need off under Testimonials, Demo & Help, All Blocks. They leave the inserter and the switcher, and the parent block stays available.</p><h3>Are my reviews deleted if I remove the plugin?</h3><p>Only if you switched Delete data on uninstall on under Demo & Help, Settings. It is off by default, and deactivating the plugin never deletes anything.</p><h3>Where do the poll results go?</h3><p>They are stored on your own site and summarised under Testimonials, Feedback & NPS Poll. The most recent 5,000 votes are kept, and nothing is sent anywhere else.</p><h3>Where do I get help?</h3><p>Post on the WordPress.org support forum for the plugin, or contact bPlugins support.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="doc-article" id="whats-new" data-group="Reference & Help" data-title="What’s New">
            <h1>What’s New</h1>
            <p class="lead">The recent releases, newest first. The full changelog lives on <a href="https://wordpress.org/plugins/b-testimonials-block/#developers" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the plugin page at WordPress.org</a>.</p>
            <h2 id="wn-104">Version 1.0.4: Controls, Editor Parity and Alignment</h2>
            <h3>New controls</h3>
            <ul><li><strong>Popup</strong>: overlay colour and blur, panel background, width, padding, radius, close button, avatar and every text size on the Popup Modal Review Trigger.</li><li><strong>Poll Style</strong>: typography for the title, description, scale labels and buttons, plus button size, radius, gap, box padding, radius and shadow.</li><li><strong>Badge Score</strong>: score typography and star row size for the six badges that show a rating.</li><li><strong>FAQ Review Accordion</strong>: corner radius, background, shadow, row gap, and question and answer padding.</li><li><strong>Trust Badges</strong>: Badge Box, Badge Icon and Badge Text panels.</li><li><strong>Testimonial Form</strong>: field label, placeholder and input styling, also added to the Feedback &amp; NPS Poll.</li><li><strong>Badge Logo</strong>: a size control for the Google, Capterra, Facebook, Trustpilot and G2 marks.</li><li><strong>Alignment</strong>: the review badges and the Social Proof Toast can now be centred or right-aligned.</li><li><strong>Card</strong>: corner wash, card hover colour, header strip background, avatar ring and card corner tint.</li></ul>
            <h3>Improvements</h3>
            <ul><li>Gradient Border Grid now draws the gradient border and star badges its name promises.</li><li>The Trust Badges icon panel follows the badge repeater, one slot per badge.</li><li>The Speech Bubble tail colour pickers show the colour actually in use, with a button to hand it back to the Card panel.</li><li>Social Proof Toast ships with three notifications, so its rotation is visible as soon as it is inserted.</li></ul>
            <h3>Fixes</h3>
            <ul><li>The Trust Badges editor preview now draws the same artwork as the published page.</li><li>Corner wash and card hover now reach every card the Card panel paints.</li><li>Client Logos restores each logo’s colour on hover inside the editor, not only on the published page.</li><li>The Before / After slider releases from the pointer when the drag ends in the editor.</li><li>The mislabelled Bars &amp; Tracks control on the Feedback &amp; NPS Poll now names what it paints.</li><li>Removed the Testimonial Form’s duplicate Form Colors panel.</li></ul>
            <h2 id="wn-103">Version 1.0.3: Major Feature Expansion</h2>
            <ul><li>Grew from a single Testimonials block to thirty-nine layout blocks.</li><li>Added the Google, Trustpilot, G2, Facebook, Capterra and Verified Buyer badges.</li><li>Added Client Logos, Before / After, the Testimonial Form and the Feedback &amp; NPS Poll.</li><li>Added the Submissions dashboard with approve, reject and bulk actions.</li><li>Added the Testimonials post type for central review management.</li><li>Added the block switcher, so a layout can be changed without losing content.</li><li>Added the React Demo &amp; Help dashboard, with a first-activation redirect.</li><li>Added responsive device controls for columns and gaps, and full card, typography and colour controls.</li><li>Hardened output escaping, input sanitising and nonce checks across the admin screens and REST routes.</li></ul>
            <h2 id="wn-100">Version 1.0.0</h2>
            <p>The first public release, with a single Testimonials block.</p>
          </article>

        </div>

        <div class="article-footer" id="pager"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- ===================== TOC ===================== -->
      <aside class="toc" id="toc">
        <div class="toc-title">Table of Contents</div>
        <ul id="tocList"></ul>
      </aside>
    </main>
  </div>

  <script>
    (function () {
      "use strict";

      var articles = Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll(".doc-article"));
      var navLinks = Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll(".nav-group-items a"));
      var order = navLinks.map(function (a) { return a.getAttribute("href").slice(1); });
      var tocList = document.getElementById("tocList");
      var toc = document.getElementById("toc");
      var pager = document.getElementById("pager");
      var bcGroup = document.getElementById("bcGroup");
      var bcTitle = document.getElementById("bcTitle");
      var spy = null;

      function articleById(id) {
        return document.getElementById(id) && document.getElementById(id).classList.contains("doc-article")
          ? document.getElementById(id) : null;
      }

      function buildToc(article) {
        tocList.innerHTML = "";
        var heads = Array.prototype.filter.call(article.querySelectorAll("h2"), function (h) {
          return h.offsetParent !== null || getComputedStyle(h).display !== "none";
        });
        /* keep the column's width reserved when empty, so the content never shifts */
        toc.style.visibility = heads.length ? "" : "hidden";
        Array.prototype.forEach.call(heads, function (h) {
          var li = document.createElement("li");
          var a = document.createElement("a");
          a.href = "#" + h.id;
          a.textContent = h.textContent;
          a.addEventListener("click", function (e) {
            e.preventDefault();
            h.scrollIntoView({ behavior: "smooth" });
            history.replaceState(null, "", "#" + article.id);
          });
          li.appendChild(a);
          tocList.appendChild(li);
        });
        if (spy) spy.disconnect();
        spy = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
          entries.forEach(function (en) {
            if (en.isIntersecting) {
              tocList.querySelectorAll("a").forEach(function (a) {
                a.classList.toggle("active", a.getAttribute("href") === "#" + en.target.id);
              });
            }
          });
        }, { rootMargin: "-80px 0px -70% 0px", threshold: 0 });
        Array.prototype.forEach.call(heads, function (h) { spy.observe(h); });
      }

      function buildPager(id) {
        pager.innerHTML = "";
        var i = order.indexOf(id);
        if (i > 0) {
          var prev = document.createElement("a");
          prev.className = "pager-link"; prev.href = "#" + order[i - 1];
          prev.setAttribute("data-nav", "");
          prev.innerHTML = '<span class="pl-dir">&larr; Previous</span><span class="pl-title">' +
            articleById(order[i - 1]).getAttribute("data-title") + "</span>";
          pager.appendChild(prev);
        }
        if (i > -1 && i < order.length - 1) {
          var next = document.createElement("a");
          next.className = "pager-link next"; next.href = "#" + order[i + 1];
          next.setAttribute("data-nav", "");
          next.innerHTML = '<span class="pl-dir">Next &rarr;</span><span class="pl-title">' +
            articleById(order[i + 1]).getAttribute("data-title") + "</span>";
          pager.appendChild(next);
        }
      }

      function show(id, scroll) {
        var article = articleById(id) || articleById("introduction");
        id = article.id;
        articles.forEach(function (a) { a.classList.toggle("active", a === article); });
        navLinks.forEach(function (a) {
          var on = a.getAttribute("href") === "#" + id;
          a.classList.toggle("active", on);
          if (on) a.closest(".nav-group").classList.add("open");
        });
        bcGroup.textContent = article.getAttribute("data-group");
        bcTitle.textContent = article.getAttribute("data-title");
        /* PER-PLUGIN: rewrite this suffix when lifting the shell for another plugin */
        document.title = article.getAttribute("data-title") + " | Testimonials Block Docs";
        buildToc(article);
        buildPager(id);
        if (scroll !== false) window.scrollTo({ top: 0 });
        document.body.classList.remove("nav-open");
      }

      document.addEventListener("click", function (e) {
        var a = e.target.closest("a[data-nav]");
        if (!a) return;
        var id = a.getAttribute("href").slice(1);
        if (!articleById(id)) return;
        e.preventDefault();
        history.pushState(null, "", "#" + id);
        show(id);
      });

      window.addEventListener("popstate", function () {
        show(location.hash.slice(1) || "introduction", false);
      });

      document.querySelectorAll(".nav-group-head").forEach(function (btn) {
        btn.addEventListener("click", function () {
          btn.parentElement.classList.toggle("open");
        });
      });

      /* Mobile nav */
      document.getElementById("menuBtn").addEventListener("click", function () {
        document.body.classList.toggle("nav-open");
      });
      document.getElementById("overlay").addEventListener("click", function () {
        document.body.classList.remove("nav-open");
      });

      /* Copy buttons */
      document.querySelectorAll(".codeblock").forEach(function (block) {
        var btn = document.createElement("button");
        btn.className = "copy-btn"; btn.type = "button"; btn.textContent = "Copy";
        btn.addEventListener("click", function () {
          var text = block.querySelector("code").innerText;
          (navigator.clipboard ? navigator.clipboard.writeText(text) : Promise.reject())
            .then(function () { btn.textContent = "Copied!"; })
            .catch(function () {
              var ta = document.createElement("textarea");
              ta.value = text; document.body.appendChild(ta); ta.select();
              document.execCommand("copy"); document.body.removeChild(ta);
              btn.textContent = "Copied!";
            });
          setTimeout(function () { btn.textContent = "Copy"; }, 1600);
        });
        block.appendChild(btn);
      });

      /* Editor switcher: Gutenberg/Classic/Elementor instruction variants, synced across pages */
      var EDITORS = [
        { id: "gutenberg", label: "Gutenberg", icon: '<rect x="3" y="3" width="7" height="7" rx="1"/><rect x="14" y="3" width="7" height="7" rx="1"/><rect x="3" y="14" width="7" height="7" rx="1"/><rect x="14" y="14" width="7" height="7" rx="1"/>' },
        { id: "classic", label: "Classic Editor", icon: '<path d="M12 20h9"/><path d="M16.5 3.5a2.1 2.1 0 0 1 3 3L7 19l-4 1 1-4z"/>' },
        { id: "elementor", label: "Elementor", icon: '<path d="M4 3h3v18H4z"/><path d="M10 3h10v4H10z"/><path d="M10 10h10v4H10z"/><path d="M10 17h10v4H10z"/>' }
      ];
      document.querySelectorAll("[data-editor-switch]").forEach(function (row) {
        row.classList.add("switch-row");
        row.innerHTML = '<span class="switch-label">Show instructions for:</span>' +
          '<div class="editor-switch" role="tablist" aria-label="Editor">' +
          EDITORS.map(function (ed) {
            return '<button type="button" data-editor="' + ed.id + '">' +
              '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" ' +
              'stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">' + ed.icon + '</svg>' + ed.label + '</button>';
          }).join("") + "</div>";
      });
      /* Tag each block with the h2 section it belongs to, so hiding a heading hides its whole section */
      document.querySelectorAll(".doc-article").forEach(function (art) {
        var current = null;
        Array.prototype.forEach.call(art.children, function (el) {
          if (el.tagName === "H2") { current = el.id || null; return; }
          if (current) el.setAttribute("data-section", current);
        });
      });
      function applySectionVisibility() {
        document.querySelectorAll(".doc-article h2[id]").forEach(function (h) {
          var hidden = getComputedStyle(h).display === "none";
          document.querySelectorAll('[data-section="' + h.id + '"]').forEach(function (el) {
            el.classList.toggle("section-hidden", hidden);
          });
        });
      }
      function setDocsEditor(mode) {
        if (!EDITORS.some(function (ed) { return ed.id === mode; })) mode = "gutenberg";
        document.body.classList.remove("editor-classic", "editor-gutenberg", "editor-elementor");
        document.body.classList.add("editor-" + mode);
        /* PER-PLUGIN: namespace this key per plugin */
        try { localStorage.setItem("bpbtbDocsEditor", mode); } catch (e) { }
        document.querySelectorAll(".editor-switch button").forEach(function (b) {
          b.classList.toggle("on", b.getAttribute("data-editor") === mode);
        });
        applySectionVisibility();
      }
      document.addEventListener("click", function (e) {
        var b = e.target.closest(".editor-switch button");
        if (!b) return;
        setDocsEditor(b.getAttribute("data-editor"));
        var active = document.querySelector(".doc-article.active");
        if (active) buildToc(active);
      });
      var savedEditor = "gutenberg";
      try { savedEditor = localStorage.getItem("bpbtbDocsEditor") || "gutenberg"; } catch (e) { }
      setDocsEditor(savedEditor);

      /* Media slots: show the real image/video when the file exists, a labeled placeholder otherwise */
      var PH_ICONS = {
        image: '<svg class="ph-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="3" y="3" width="18" height="18" rx="2"/><circle cx="9" cy="9" r="2"/><path d="m21 15-3.1-3.1a2 2 0 0 0-2.8 0L6 21"/></svg>',
        video: '<svg class="ph-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="2" y="4" width="20" height="16" rx="2"/><path d="m10 9 5 3-5 3z"/></svg>'
      };
      function youtubeId(src) {
        var m = src.match(/(?:youtube(?:-nocookie)?\.com\/(?:watch\?[^#]*v=|embed\/|shorts\/|live\/)|youtu\.be\/)([A-Za-z0-9_-]{6,})/);
        return m ? m[1] : null;
      }
      document.querySelectorAll(".media-slot").forEach(function (slot) {
        var src = (slot.getAttribute("data-src") || "").trim();
        var type = slot.getAttribute("data-media") || "image";
        var caption = slot.getAttribute("data-caption") || "";
        var isRemote = /^https?:\/\//i.test(src);
        function renderPlaceholder() {
          slot.innerHTML = "";
          var box = document.createElement("div");
          box.className = "media-ph";
          box.innerHTML = PH_ICONS[type] || PH_ICONS.image;
          var label = document.createElement("div");
          label.className = "ph-label"; label.textContent = caption;
          var path = document.createElement("div");
          path.className = "ph-path"; path.textContent = src;
          var hint = document.createElement("div");
          hint.className = "ph-hint";
          hint.textContent = type === "video"
            ? "Placeholder: paste the YouTube link into this slot's data-src and it will embed here"
            : "Placeholder: paste the CDN image URL into this slot's data-src and it will appear here";
          box.appendChild(label); box.appendChild(path); box.appendChild(hint);
          slot.appendChild(box);
        }
        function renderMedia(el) {
          slot.innerHTML = "";
          slot.appendChild(el);
          if (caption) {
            var fc = document.createElement("figcaption");
            fc.textContent = caption;
            slot.appendChild(fc);
          }
        }
        renderPlaceholder();
        if (!src) return;
        var yid = type === "video" ? youtubeId(src) : null;
        if (yid) {
          var wrap = document.createElement("div");
          wrap.className = "yt-wrap";
          var fr = document.createElement("iframe");
          fr.src = "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/" + yid;
          fr.title = caption;
          fr.loading = "lazy";
          fr.allow = "accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share";
          fr.allowFullscreen = true;
          wrap.appendChild(fr);
          renderMedia(wrap);
        } else if (type === "video") {
          if (!isRemote && src.indexOf("/") === -1) return;
          var v = document.createElement("video");
          v.controls = true; v.preload = "metadata";
          v.addEventListener("loadedmetadata", function () { renderMedia(v); });
          v.addEventListener("error", renderPlaceholder);
          v.src = src;
        } else {
          var im = new Image();
          im.addEventListener("load", function () { im.alt = caption; renderMedia(im); });
          im.addEventListener("error", renderPlaceholder);
          im.src = src;
        }
      });

      /* Image lightbox: click any screenshot to view it full size */
      var lb = document.createElement("div");
      lb.className = "lightbox";
      lb.setAttribute("role", "dialog");
      lb.setAttribute("aria-modal", "true");
      lb.setAttribute("aria-label", "Image viewer");
      lb.innerHTML =
        '<button class="lb-close" type="button" aria-label="Close (Esc)">' +
        '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M18 6 6 18M6 6l12 12"/></svg></button>' +
        '<button class="lb-prev" type="button" aria-label="Previous image">' +
        '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m15 18-6-6 6-6"/></svg></button>' +
        '<button class="lb-next" type="button" aria-label="Next image">' +
        '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m9 18 6-6-6-6"/></svg></button>' +
        '<img alt=""><div class="lb-caption"></div><div class="lb-count"></div>';
      document.body.appendChild(lb);
      var lbImg = lb.querySelector("img");
      var lbCap = lb.querySelector(".lb-caption");
      var lbCount = lb.querySelector(".lb-count");
      var lbGroup = [], lbIndex = 0, lbReturnFocus = null;

      function lbShow(i) {
        if (!lbGroup.length) return;
        lbIndex = (i + lbGroup.length) % lbGroup.length;
        var img = lbGroup[lbIndex];
        lbImg.src = img.currentSrc || img.src;
        lbImg.alt = img.alt || "";
        var cap = img.closest(".media-slot");
        cap = cap && cap.querySelector("figcaption");
        lbCap.textContent = cap ? cap.textContent : (img.alt || "");
        lbCount.textContent = lbGroup.length > 1 ? lbIndex + 1 + " / " + lbGroup.length : "";
        lb.classList.toggle("single", lbGroup.length < 2);
      }
      function lbOpen(img) {
        var article = img.closest(".doc-article") || document;
        lbGroup = Array.prototype.filter.call(
          article.querySelectorAll(".media-slot img"),
          function (el) { return el.offsetParent !== null; }
        );
        var start = lbGroup.indexOf(img);
        if (start === -1) { lbGroup = [img]; start = 0; }
        lbReturnFocus = document.activeElement;
        lb.classList.add("open");
        document.body.classList.add("lb-open");
        lbShow(start);
        lb.querySelector(".lb-close").focus();
      }
      function lbClose() {
        lb.classList.remove("open");
        document.body.classList.remove("lb-open");
        lbImg.removeAttribute("src");
        if (lbReturnFocus && lbReturnFocus.focus) lbReturnFocus.focus();
      }

      document.addEventListener("click", function (e) {
        var img = e.target.closest(".media-slot img");
        if (img) { e.preventDefault(); lbOpen(img); return; }
        if (!lb.classList.contains("open")) return;
        if (e.target.closest(".lb-close")) return lbClose();
        if (e.target.closest(".lb-prev")) return lbShow(lbIndex - 1);
        if (e.target.closest(".lb-next")) return lbShow(lbIndex + 1);
        if (e.target === lb) lbClose();
      });
      document.addEventListener("keydown", function (e) {
        if (!lb.classList.contains("open")) return;
        if (e.key === "Escape") { e.preventDefault(); lbClose(); }
        else if (e.key === "ArrowLeft" && lbGroup.length > 1) { e.preventDefault(); lbShow(lbIndex - 1); }
        else if (e.key === "ArrowRight" && lbGroup.length > 1) { e.preventDefault(); lbShow(lbIndex + 1); }
        else if (e.key === "Tab") {
          var f = lb.querySelectorAll("button:not([style*='display: none'])");
          var list = Array.prototype.filter.call(f, function (b) { return b.offsetParent !== null; });
          if (!list.length) return;
          var i = list.indexOf(document.activeElement);
          e.preventDefault();
          list[(i + (e.shiftKey ? -1 : 1) + list.length) % list.length].focus();
        }
      });

      /* Search */
      var input = document.getElementById("searchInput");
      var results = document.getElementById("searchResults");
      var index = articles.map(function (a) {
        return {
          id: a.id,
          title: a.getAttribute("data-title"),
          group: a.getAttribute("data-group"),
          heads: Array.prototype.map.call(a.querySelectorAll("h2"), function (h) { return h.textContent; }),
          text: a.textContent.replace(/\s+/g, " ").toLowerCase()
        };
      });

      function runSearch() {
        var q = input.value.trim().toLowerCase();
        results.innerHTML = "";
        if (q.length < 2) { results.classList.remove("open"); return; }
        var hits = index.map(function (it) {
          var score = 0;
          if (it.title.toLowerCase().indexOf(q) > -1) score = 3;
          else if (it.heads.some(function (h) { return h.toLowerCase().indexOf(q) > -1; })) score = 2;
          else if (it.text.indexOf(q) > -1) score = 1;
          return { it: it, score: score };
        }).filter(function (r) { return r.score > 0; })
          .sort(function (a, b) { return b.score - a.score; })
          .map(function (r) { return r.it; })
          .slice(0, 8);
        if (!hits.length) {
          results.innerHTML = '<div class="empty">No results for &ldquo;' + q.replace(/</g, "&lt;") + '&rdquo;</div>';
        } else {
          hits.forEach(function (it) {
            var a = document.createElement("a");
            a.href = "#" + it.id;
            a.innerHTML = '<div class="r-title">' + it.title + '</div><div class="r-crumb">Docs / ' + it.group + "</div>";
            a.addEventListener("click", function (e) {
              e.preventDefault();
              history.pushState(null, "", "#" + it.id);
              show(it.id);
              input.value = ""; results.classList.remove("open");
            });
            results.appendChild(a);
          });
        }
        results.classList.add("open");
      }

      input.addEventListener("input", runSearch);
      input.addEventListener("focus", runSearch);
      input.addEventListener("keydown", function (e) {
        var items = results.querySelectorAll("a");
        var sel = results.querySelector("a.sel");
        var i = Array.prototype.indexOf.call(items, sel);
        if (e.key === "ArrowDown" && items.length) {
          e.preventDefault();
          if (sel) sel.classList.remove("sel");
          items[(i + 1) % items.length].classList.add("sel");
        } else if (e.key === "ArrowUp" && items.length) {
          e.preventDefault();
          if (sel) sel.classList.remove("sel");
          items[(i - 1 + items.length) % items.length].classList.add("sel");
        } else if (e.key === "Enter" && sel) {
          e.preventDefault(); sel.click();
        } else if (e.key === "Escape") {
          results.classList.remove("open"); input.blur();
        }
      });
      document.addEventListener("click", function (e) {
        if (!e.target.closest(".search-box")) results.classList.remove("open");
      });
      document.addEventListener("keydown", function (e) {
        if (e.key === "/" && document.activeElement !== input &&
          !/^(INPUT|TEXTAREA)$/.test(document.activeElement.tagName)) {
          e.preventDefault(); input.focus();
        }
      });

      show(location.hash.slice(1) || "introduction", false);
    })();
  </script>
</body>

</html>
